WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.720
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 4 of CME 285.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.080
So today is a very
special day because we'll

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.960
be talking about reinforcement
learning-based training

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.540
methods that are actually
getting quite popular,

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.600
especially when it comes
to post-training LLMs.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.920
And the reason why today's
session is a little bit special

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.200
is because contrary
to the previous ones,

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.600
there is going to be a
little bit of math involved,

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.600
which is why last time we said
all the RL-based methods we will

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.460
just reserve them for today.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.280
So today is the day.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.960
And yeah, so I wanted to
start with this disclaimer.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.120
And the second thing
I wanted to say

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:58.160
is we will try to start from
intuition first and then stay

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.810
rigorous on the math.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:03.410
But please don't be intimidated
by the big formulas.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.730
Yeah, hope it's going
to be a good one.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:10.370
With that, we're going to
start as usual by recapping

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.010
what we saw last time.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.410
So if you remember,
last time we were

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.530
talking about training LLMs.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:24.650
And the first step of training
LLMs is called pretraining.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:28.290
It is a step that is meant
to teach the model how

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.090
to understand texts,
how to understand codes.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.250
And this step typically
involves training your model

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:37.890
on a lot of data.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.330
By a lot of data, I mean
trillions of tokens.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:47.210
So there's a lot of work around
finding the right mixtures, how

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.210
much web should it be in
there, how the books should

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.450
be in there, and so on.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.690
And the loss
function for this one

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:58.700
is the cross-entropy
loss, which tries

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.180
to penalize any
deviation from the token

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:06.900
that the model needs to predict,
so its next token prediction.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:12.100
And we saw some emerging stages,
such as mid training which

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:17.120
involves higher quality data
and also long context training,

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.340
which involves training your
model on longer contexts data

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.700
as well.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.500
So that's the first step.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.900
The second step that we saw was
called Supervised Fine Tuning,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:29.620
a.k.a.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.060
SFT.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:35.460
And in this step, we're
trying to take our model that

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.860
understands text, that
understands code, and tune it

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.540
to be useful.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.000
So what do I mean by useful?

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.860
For instance, if you want
to have an assistant,

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:50.700
you would want your model to
give answers to questions.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:56.140
And that is one of the examples
where people would use SFT for.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.670
So in terms of
the loss function,

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.230
we still have the cross entropy.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:05.950
Except here we're only
trying to penalize deviations

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:09.750
for token predictions that
are on the output side.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.550
So it's next token
prediction but for outputs.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:18.050
At the end of this stage,
our model understand text.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.670
Our model is useful.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:24.290
But here we may want to
inject human preferences.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:28.910
And we saw this third step
called preference tuning.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:33.070
And this is the step that
involves RL-based methods

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.230
that we're going to see today.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:41.870
We did not see any specifics
about the exact loss formula.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.270
So we're going to see today.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.070
And the other piece
that we saw was the fact

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:53.030
of increasing the model's
reasoning capabilities, so

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:57.320
reasoning, which also
happens to be performed

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.760
with this RL-based techniques.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.000
And we're also going
to see that today.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.880
And this is where we
left off last time.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.680
And today, we're going to
focus on these last two stages,

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:17.240
and in particular, emphasize how
we come up with the loss, which

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.000
right now, we don't
know what the loss is

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.960
and see how we can use that.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.800
Cool.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.000
I want to, again, have
a big disclaimer here

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.400
that these methods, they
are typically mathematically

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.860
involved, not just
for this lecture,

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.440
but if you were to read
papers on that topic.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.040
And so if you feel
like it's too much,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.380
here is the proposed
steps for remediation.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.360
So first one, focus on
the intuition on why

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.240
a certain thing is needed.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.770
Second one is if there
are formulas or symbols

00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:01.290
that you are not familiar with,
just make sure to review them.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.210
And then the third one is
just to interpret the formulas

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.970
from first principles.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.890
And I actually have
a second disclaimer,

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:14.250
which is that on top
of all of that, papers,

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:18.130
they tend to choose conventions
that are different from one

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:19.570
paper to another.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:22.410
So that may add
to the confusion.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.970
So we will see that,
for instance, we're

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.330
going to talk about rewards.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.690
Rewards are usually denoted r.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.890
But sometimes in
papers, r could also

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:36.890
mean a ratio, a ratio
of probabilities.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:40.590
So here, to make sure we
distinguish them properly,

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:43.890
we will note them
rho in this class.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:49.410
Second one is the policy, and
we will see what a policy is.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.870
There are several
kinds of policy.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.230
Is it the old policies,
the reference policy?

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:59.410
And sometimes these
notations are also mixed.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.650
And then the third
one to call out

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:05.130
is that so far,
we've been focusing

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.970
on minimizing a loss function.

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:13.090
And we noted the loss
function L. Well, here

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:17.455
most of the time we will
want to maximize quantities.

00:06:17.455 --> 00:06:19.330
But the problem is
sometimes these quantities

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:24.190
are also noted L which usually
you think of as being a loss.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.830
But just make sure that you
know what you want to do.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:28.830
Do you want to minimize?

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.050
You want to maximize?

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:32.070
So that's also another
thing to keep in mind.

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:35.930
Cool.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.530
Well, with all of that
said, let's start.

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:43.090
And I wanted to first start
with a refresher on what

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:45.130
reinforcement learning is.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.850
So I'm not sure if any of you
has ever taken a reinforcement

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:50.490
learning-based class.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.580
But at a very high
level, the way it works

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:57.580
is you have an agent
that is interacting

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:01.780
with an environment
that is in a given state

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:07.900
and that wonders what action
to take to go to another state.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:13.700
So the way it tries to decide
on which action to take

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:18.300
is something that is
leveraging a policy.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:23.420
So pi theta is typically
how it's denoted.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:30.540
And after the agent performs an
action, it receives a reward.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.380
And the goal of
RL-based problems

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.580
is to maximize
the sum of rewards

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:39.580
that your agent is collecting.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.180
Now you may be
wondering, well, how

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.860
does that have to do with LLMs?

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.940
So here, we're going
to see a little bit how

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.870
things map to the LLM world.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:08:00.150
So the agent here is the LLM
that takes some input as a state

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.850
and tries to decide
what to do next,

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:05.290
so what token to sample next.

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:08.550
So the action is a token.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:13.710
And in order to decide
what token to go next to,

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:18.430
what it does is it relies on
the probability distribution

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:20.830
that it outputs, given an input.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:24.170
And so here that one
will be noted pi theta.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.290
So back to the
notation last time,

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:31.590
so this is where the pi is
coming from, pi as in policy.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:34.909
And it collects rewards.

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:40.830
So one note here is that it
can be challenging to define

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.270
token-level rewards.

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:48.600
So a lot of the time, we will
also encounter this setup

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.800
not at the token level,
but at the response level

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.400
or at the completion level.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.680
And so here you
can also consider

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:01.840
an input as being
your prompt, an action

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:06.920
as being your
response, and pi theta

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.120
as being just the probability
of you outputting a response,

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.600
given your prompt.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:17.920
And then as a result of
that, you will get a reward.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:21.840
So we're going to spend a few
minutes on the reward part.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.040
And I know we spent
maybe 10 minutes

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:29.140
last time talking about
this in different settings.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:33.440
So here I just want to
summarize what we saw.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:39.480
So in order for you to quantify
how good a response is,

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:44.080
you actually have typically
two different categories

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.250
of situations you can be in.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:48.890
The first one,
the first category

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.610
is where there is
no absolute ground

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:55.730
truth to the answer, where
actually judging your answer

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:58.730
is a little bit more subjective.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:03.890
And this is where we saw in
the preference tuning case

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:08.250
where we could have
human preferences that

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:13.830
try to up rank, let's say,
responses that have, let's say,

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:19.210
a better tone compared to
ones that have a worse tone.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:23.210
And this is something that
you would typically model.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:27.770
You would model with
a reward model that

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:33.930
leverages what we saw last time,
the Bradley-Terry formulation,

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:38.970
which is a way to express your
loss in a pairwise fashion

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:42.330
and train a reward model.

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:46.180
So here the first category is
you don't have a ground truth.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:48.020
And you try to
come up with a way

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:50.840
to quantify how good
responses are, for instance,

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.180
with human preferences.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:56.500
And you would use the
Bradley-Terry formulation,

00:10:56.500 --> 00:11:02.200
and these methods
would be called RLHF,

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:06.340
because you would use human
feedback as a way to quantify

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:10.460
how good the responses are.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:15.380
And then the second big category
of situations you can be into

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:21.980
is where you can
actually verifiably tell

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.260
if your answer is
correct or not.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:27.600
And so here are some
examples of situations

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.100
could be if you want to
solve a math problem,

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.880
you know what the solution is.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:35.200
Or if you want to write
a piece of software,

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:37.380
you know if your
solution is correct

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:42.260
or not if, let's say, it
passes the test cases.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:48.110
So you have a deterministic way
to check whether your answer is

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:49.830
correct or not.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:53.590
And so these methods
you will also find them

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:57.470
under the name RLVR,
Reinforcement Learning

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:00.470
with Verifiable Rewards.

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:03.110
And these are
typically in the field

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:09.350
of reasoning, so math
problems and coding problems.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:13.370
So this is how you
can have your rewards.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:15.790
And so it would be more
at the completion level,

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:20.150
not at the token level,
at the completion level.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:24.370
So given this
definition of reward,

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:29.870
you may want to tweak the
parameters of your model

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:33.150
to maximize the rewards
that you're getting.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:35.710
So coming back to the
examples that we saw last week

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.590
in the preference tuning
case, what you want

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:42.300
is to align your model
with human preferences.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.640
So you would start
with a prompt,

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:50.440
and then you would generate
a completion with your LLM.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.900
And then given the completion
that you would generate,

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:56.440
you would pass in the
prompt and the completion

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:59.400
into a reward model
that is frozen

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:02.520
that tells you whether
the answer is good or not

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:05.240
with respect to the prompt.

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:10.320
And depending on how the
reward model responds,

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.520
you would tweak the
parameters of your LLM

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:17.560
in order to maximize the
rewards, the expected rewards

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:20.560
that is generated.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:22.220
So this is the
preference tuning case

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:26.560
and you have similarly
for the reasoning case,

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:31.360
when you have a prompt that is
maybe more of a reasoning prompt

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:33.240
or a coding prompt.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:36.760
And here you have
a deterministic way

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:39.500
to evaluate the
quality of your answer.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:44.570
And so here you would only
look at the final answer.

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:48.090
And you would use
that in order to tweak

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:51.950
the parameters of your LLM
which is here, your policy.

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:55.410
OK.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:58.390
So now we talked
about the setting.

00:13:58.390 --> 00:13:59.750
Now let's go into the math.

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:00.590
Yeah?

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:01.930
[INAUDIBLE]

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:05.090
So the question is, would the
probability of a full response

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:06.690
be very low?

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:08.190
Yes, indeed it would.

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:10.530
That is the reason why
people use logarithm

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:12.670
in order to have some
numerical stability.

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.810
But yeah, great point.

00:14:14.810 --> 00:14:15.790
Yeah?

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:17.210
[INAUDIBLE]

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:19.530
So the question is, do you
need a human every time

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:20.910
you generate a completion?

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:24.810
So the answer is no, which is
the reason why you're actually

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:28.450
training a reward
model firsthand.

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:31.210
You're training it
at human preferences.

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:34.050
And the reason why you're doing
that is because you don't want

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:36.490
to have a human in the
loop every time you

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:37.930
generate a completion.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:41.340
And what you would do is
to use that reward model

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:44.100
for any responses that
you're generating,

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:45.900
and you're using
that reward model

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.720
in order to assess how
good the response is.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:52.600
So yeah, you are actually not
leveraging humans in this loop.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:55.860
OK.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:57.180
Perfect.

00:14:57.180 --> 00:15:00.380
So I was saying let's
go into the math.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:08.220
So here, our objective function
is to maximize the expected

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.660
rewards that you're getting.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:14.100
So in mathematical terms,
what you want to do

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:18.980
is to find parameters
theta of your model

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:22.540
that maximize the
objective function, which

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:30.380
is the expectation of rewards
as you're sampling trajectories

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:36.100
from your policy pi theta,
which is your model.

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:41.110
And so I know what
you're thinking.

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:42.790
In machine learning,
when you want

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:44.770
to optimize an
objective function,

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:48.350
typically what you want
to get is the gradient

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:50.590
because you want to
perform your updates.

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:53.030
So the first question that
we want to tackle here

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:58.710
is how do you get the
gradients of this objective?

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:00.710
And so I know a lot
of us are coming

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:02.210
from different backgrounds.

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:05.590
And I want to make sure we're
speaking the same language.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:10.510
So little refresher
on gradients.

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:14.350
You can think of
gradients as a way for you

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:19.270
to get the direction
and the magnitude

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:22.670
of the steepest increase.

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:29.730
So in the 1D case where your
function has an input x,

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:35.070
that is just one number,
you all know the derivative.

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.220
So if the derivative
is positive,

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:40.460
what that means is your function
increases as x increases.

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:45.520
If it's negative, it
increases as x decreases.

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:50.560
So the generalization of that
for the n dimensional case

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:52.400
is the gradient.

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:56.120
And the gradient
of f in this case

00:16:56.120 --> 00:17:00.120
is the partial derivative of
that function with respect

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:05.300
to all the dimensions of
your input x, which is in Rn.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:10.140
Are we all good on this?

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:10.640
OK.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:14.640
So with that being
said, let's go back

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:18.440
to our original problem, which
is we have an objective function

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:20.040
to maximize.

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.880
And what we want to do is
to compute the gradient.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:28.720
And so I will just do that.

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:37.880
So what we want is for us to
maximize the expected rewards.

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:42.280
And so in mathematical
terms, what that means is

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:52.080
we want to maximize
the rewards such

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:55.760
that the trajectory is
sampled from your policy.

00:17:55.760 --> 00:18:00.240
So this is exactly what
is on the slide means.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:05.880
And so if you want to
take the gradient of that,

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:13.780
well, you would just compute
what is in between the integral.

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:19.980
So what you would do is
just wander for a second

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:21.660
what is a function of theta.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:23.020
So is R a function of theta?

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:24.040
No, it is frozen.

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:28.040
Is pi theta a function of theta?

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:29.360
Yes.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:33.450
So what you would do is
actually go and put the gradient

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.210
in there.

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:38.250
And of course, you
have some regularity

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:44.150
that you assume is in place.

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:44.650
OK.

00:18:44.650 --> 00:18:46.290
Mathematical trick.

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:49.770
What is the gradient
of pi of theta?

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:55.690
So you may know that the
gradient of log of a function

00:18:55.690 --> 00:19:00.970
is the gradient of that
function over that function.

00:19:00.970 --> 00:19:03.330
So that's a mathematical result.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.530
So what that means is that
the gradient of a function

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:12.650
is equal to the gradient of
the log of that function times

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:14.890
that function.

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:21.810
So here this is equal to the
gradient of the log of pi theta

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:25.390
times pi theta.

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:32.260
And so what does that mean?

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:34.060
I'm going to just
rewrite everything

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:35.560
so that we're all clear.

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:51.060
So it's integral of R tau
gradient of log of pi theta pi

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:56.980
theta of t and d tau.

00:19:56.980 --> 00:19:59.680
So this is nothing else.

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:12.220
This is nothing else than the
expected value of your rewards

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:25.300
times the gradient of the
logarithm of pi theta,

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:31.690
where tau is sampled from your
policy of parameters theta.

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:37.170
Does everyone agree
with the math?

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:37.670
Yes?

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:39.070
[INAUDIBLE]

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:41.590
So the question is, how
did you introduce the log?

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:48.950
So I just used the
fact that the gradient

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:51.150
of the log of a
function phi of theta

00:20:51.150 --> 00:21:01.130
is equal to the gradient of that
function over that function.

00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:03.790
So it's just a
mathematical identity.

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:07.070
[INAUDIBLE]

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:07.850
Yes, exactly.

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:11.950
So what we did is we
just put that here.

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:14.310
[INAUDIBLE]

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:15.610
Yes, exactly.

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:17.930
Thank you.

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:18.430
Great.

00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:20.050
Does everyone agree
with the math?

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:25.590
OK, cool.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:28.190
So the reason why I'm doing
the math on the blackboard

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:30.920
is not because I like writing
on the blackboard, but I mean,

00:21:30.920 --> 00:21:31.420
I do.

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:35.400
But here you will see
this formula quite a lot.

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:40.120
And it's just important to
know where this comes from.

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:43.760
And so what that means
is that the gradient

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:49.920
of your objective function
is incentivizing your model

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:54.440
to increase probabilities
of actions that

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:58.680
have a positive
reward and decrease

00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:03.740
the probability of actions
that have a negative reward.

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:04.240
Yeah?

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:05.800
[INAUDIBLE]

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:10.560
So the question is, what does
the tilde in the tau tilde

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:11.920
pi of theta mean?

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:16.200
So that means that you're
sampling your trajectory

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:19.240
from the probability
distribution given

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:21.640
by your policy of
parameters theta.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:24.100
So tilde means sampled from.

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:32.950
So again, I will just
repeat the intuition.

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:35.450
So what you can take
away from this formula

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:41.210
is that if the
reward is positive,

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:44.050
the gradient will
incentivize your model

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:49.270
to increase the probability
of such actions.

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:52.450
And it will
incentivize your model

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:54.810
to decrease the
probability of actions

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:59.510
that have negative reward.

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:00.010
OK.

00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:04.430
So based on that,
there's one little thing

00:23:04.430 --> 00:23:05.930
that I want to tell
you about, which

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:10.570
is that we talked in
terms of trajectories.

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:15.450
And in the LLM case, what we
typically do is we first have

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:18.770
a set of prompts, the x's.

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:26.100
And then we sample the responses
from the policy of our model,

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:30.180
given these prompts.

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:35.140
So the reason why we only did
the tau sample from pi theta

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:39.220
is just because it didn't
want to make it more

00:23:39.220 --> 00:23:40.440
confusing on the blackboard.

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:45.500
But just think of expectations
as being over prompts and then

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:50.000
over responses,
given these prompts.

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:50.500
OK.

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:55.020
So now you may wonder,
well, how can you

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:58.900
update the weights of your
model given what we saw?

00:23:58.900 --> 00:24:03.180
So here what we would do is
we would have a prompt x,

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:05.460
and we would
generate a completion

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:14.780
y sampled from our
LLM, given the input x.

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:16.940
What we would do is
we would evaluate

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:20.180
the reward that is
associated with the prompt

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:22.780
and the response.

00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:26.630
And then we would compute
the gradient, which we saw

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:31.730
is R times the gradient
of the log of pi.

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:35.990
And so here's exactly
what's on the slides.

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:38.710
And then you would
update your parameters.

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:41.850
So you may be familiar
with gradient descent.

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:46.050
So it's theta minus, but here we
want to maximize the objective.

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:51.450
We want to maximize our
model generating high reward.

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:56.110
So here it's actually theta
plus alpha times the gradient

00:24:56.110 --> 00:24:57.970
because it's gradient ascent.

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:08.090
So one thing that I want to say
is that contrary to pretraining,

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:14.630
contrary to SFT, here we need
to always generate the response.

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:16.850
Because here we're in
an on policy setting.

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:19.230
We're generating the
response and then

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:22.770
performing the update on the
response generated by the model.

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:24.300
This is not a fixed response.

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:26.480
It is generated by the model.

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:28.880
So the problem
with that is you're

00:25:28.880 --> 00:25:31.560
asking your model to
generate responses every time

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:34.640
you want to make an update.

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:38.160
And that step is very expensive.

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:40.400
You cannot parallelize it
because you need to ask

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:42.380
your model, OK, what
is the next token?

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:45.500
And then you take that token and
you ask, what is the next token?

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:50.760
And one at a time you're
generating these responses.

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:53.840
And so one thing that
I want to tell you

00:25:53.840 --> 00:26:00.680
is a method that
people can use in order

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:03.480
to limit the number
of times they

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:05.900
need to generate completions.

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:10.620
And I want to write that down.

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:17.240
So here what we're
saying is that we

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:21.810
want to maximize the
expected rewards when

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:26.650
the trajectory is sampled
from the current policy.

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:28.350
So we want to maximize this.

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:38.770
So we want to maximize the
expectation of R of tau

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:44.750
when R, sorry, when tau is
sampled from the current policy.

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:50.170
Which is equal to
the integral of R

00:26:50.170 --> 00:26:57.570
of tau pi theta of tau d tau.

00:26:57.570 --> 00:27:05.090
But another way of writing this
could be to say if we have,

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:13.530
let's say, another
policy tau fixed,

00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:20.620
we could express this quantity
as a function of tau fixed

00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:23.880
by letting this be popped up.

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:36.100
So here we do pi theta of tau
over pi theta fixed of tau times

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:43.320
pi theta fixed of tau d tau.

00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:47.540
Which is equal to
the expectation

00:27:47.540 --> 00:28:07.840
of R of tau times pi theta of
tau over pi theta fixed of tau.

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:15.260
Where tau is sampled
from pi theta fixed.

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:17.040
So I want to just highlight.

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:24.670
So we went from maximizing
the expected value when

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:31.310
tau is sampled from
pi theta of a quantity

00:28:31.310 --> 00:28:37.710
over to a quantity which samples
the trajectory from a policy

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:43.270
of fixed given parameters.

00:28:43.270 --> 00:28:46.430
So why did I do that?

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:48.990
So let's imagine for
a second that we're

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:52.270
tired of always generating
completions every time we

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:54.510
want to perform an update.

00:28:54.510 --> 00:29:00.990
Let's assume we generate
completions, let's say once.

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:07.710
And then we perform updates
using the completions

00:29:07.710 --> 00:29:14.150
of that one time but by
reweighting the rewards

00:29:14.150 --> 00:29:17.910
for a given trajectory
with the ratio

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:21.950
of the probability of
your current policy

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:26.150
over your fixed policy.

00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:29.510
So the reason why I wrote
that is because sometimes we

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:30.810
don't want to always do that.

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:33.310
We want to have a
fixed policy that

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:38.110
does that once and then just
reweight the terms in a way that

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:41.110
makes it still
mathematically valid,

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:42.770
because this is
what we care about.

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:49.790
So one thing that
I want to add is

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:58.650
this term is called
importance sampling ratio.

00:29:58.650 --> 00:29:59.150
Yes?

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:00.510
[INAUDIBLE]

00:30:00.510 --> 00:30:02.950
So the question is I
don't get the benefits.

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:05.230
We're still computing
pi theta of tau.

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:06.310
What is the benefit?

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:12.510
So the trick is
evaluating the policy

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:15.720
is different with
generating a rollout.

00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:17.680
Because evaluating
the policy, you're

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:21.320
taking everything as input
in parallel and computing

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:23.220
everything, all
the probabilities.

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:25.100
But here when you
do the rollout,

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:27.560
as in when you
generate a response,

00:30:27.560 --> 00:30:30.920
you need to take the first
token to generate the token.

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:35.760
And then take that token
and generate the next token

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:37.840
over and over.

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:39.260
So this is expensive.

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:40.060
This is cheap.

00:30:42.820 --> 00:30:43.320
Yeah?

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:44.480
[INAUDIBLE]

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:47.000
So the question is, please
explain a bit more what

00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:48.600
you mean by this is cheap.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:50.860
So here you know
the full response.

00:30:50.860 --> 00:30:52.720
You know the full response.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:57.520
So you're putting all
the responses inputs,

00:30:57.520 --> 00:30:58.380
all in parallel.

00:30:58.380 --> 00:31:01.220
So it's all being computed
in a nice parallel fashion.

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:11.280
So maybe I can do something
else that could be more helpful.

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:13.630
You mean that [INAUDIBLE]?

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:19.630
Yeah, exactly.

00:31:22.974 --> 00:31:35.070
So here, so generation, what
you're doing is you're putting--

00:31:35.070 --> 00:31:37.490
so this is your model--

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:43.050
putting a token in, token out.

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:53.130
That token in, token
out, and so on.

00:31:53.130 --> 00:32:01.130
But for evaluation,
what you're doing is

00:32:01.130 --> 00:32:03.310
you already know
the full completion.

00:32:03.310 --> 00:32:04.310
You already know it.

00:32:04.310 --> 00:32:06.310
You know all the tokens.

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:15.252
So you're putting everything
as inputs all at once.

00:32:15.252 --> 00:32:18.760
And you know that your
LLM is a decoder only LLM,

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:21.240
so it has the masked
self-attention.

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:23.960
So it's able to nicely
only attend to itself.

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:26.900
And the ones before all
that is very parallel.

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:30.860
And you can get the
corresponding probabilities

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:31.680
as outputs.

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:36.460
This is expensive.

00:32:36.460 --> 00:32:37.560
This is cheap.

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:40.940
OK.

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:41.960
Great question.

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:42.460
Yeah?

00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:43.460
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:43.460 --> 00:32:46.420
So the question is we're still
doing the expensive calculation

00:32:46.420 --> 00:32:47.280
with theta fixed.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:51.140
Yes, but you can do it once
and reuse that several times.

00:32:51.140 --> 00:32:53.480
You don't need to
do that every time.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:54.767
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:54.767 --> 00:32:56.600
The question is for
multiple training steps.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:57.620
Yes.

00:32:57.620 --> 00:32:59.240
So we will see that
more in detail.

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:01.620
I just didn't want this
to come out of the blue.

00:33:01.620 --> 00:33:05.100
This is the reason.

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:11.630
So now, what I want to do is
to go through some challenges

00:33:11.630 --> 00:33:15.550
that such a vanilla
approach can exhibit.

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:18.130
So this is not one challenge.

00:33:18.130 --> 00:33:23.990
This is just like a small FYI.

00:33:23.990 --> 00:33:26.990
The first challenge
that I want to mention

00:33:26.990 --> 00:33:33.550
is that if we use a reward
function that does not

00:33:33.550 --> 00:33:40.630
have a concept of relative
compared to some baseline,

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:45.190
we may get gradients that
are of high variance.

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:47.070
So I'll give you an example.

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:51.510
Let's assume we have a prompt,
and we have two answers.

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:55.230
Let's say one is good
and the other one is bad.

00:33:55.230 --> 00:34:00.390
In the first case, the good
answer has a reward of 101,

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:05.470
and the bad answer
has a reward of 100.

00:34:05.470 --> 00:34:10.600
So you know that 101
is greater than 100,

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:14.320
but both are very high, and you
don't exactly get the difference

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:17.960
relative to one another.

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:20.820
The problem is that
this will lead--

00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:23.440
this will lead to
noisy gradients,

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:29.639
because you know that your
model will update parameters

00:34:29.639 --> 00:34:33.679
as a function of
R and the gradient

00:34:33.679 --> 00:34:36.199
of the log of the policy.

00:34:36.199 --> 00:34:42.280
So if R is always big
to make big updates,

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:48.320
it will make big updates to
get good answers pop up more.

00:34:48.320 --> 00:34:51.280
It will make big updates to
make the bad answer pop up

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:55.400
more but in a lower magnitude,
because the reward is

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:59.400
lower than the one for
than for the good one.

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:01.780
So at the end of the day,
if you do that enough times,

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:05.560
you will get the parameters
that you want to get,

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:09.450
but you will have very
high variance gradients.

00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:13.410
As in you will have big updates
for good, then big updates

00:35:13.410 --> 00:35:17.050
for bad and so on.

00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:20.970
So what you want
is to actually get

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:25.770
a sense of what is the expected
reward that you should receive

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:28.930
for a given state.

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:32.690
And then contrast the
reward of each action

00:35:32.690 --> 00:35:36.090
with respect to that.

00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:43.410
So if you say that OK, on
average 100.5 is roughly OK,

00:35:43.410 --> 00:35:49.510
then if you have plus 0.5,
then you know it's good.

00:35:49.510 --> 00:35:54.090
And if you have minus 0.5,
then you know it's not good.

00:35:54.090 --> 00:35:58.130
And you can show
on a toy example

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:01.810
that the gradients that
you get as a result of that

00:36:01.810 --> 00:36:05.370
are of much lower
variance when you do

00:36:05.370 --> 00:36:10.660
have that baseline, when you
do have that sense of relative

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:13.540
to something.

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:16.220
So that's the first challenge.

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:22.100
The second challenge is that the
two types of reward that we saw

00:36:22.100 --> 00:36:24.860
are at the completion
level, meaning

00:36:24.860 --> 00:36:27.340
that you either know
that everything is good

00:36:27.340 --> 00:36:29.428
or either know that
everything is bad.

00:36:29.428 --> 00:36:31.220
You either know that
everything is correct,

00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:34.420
or you know that
everything is not correct.

00:36:34.420 --> 00:36:36.440
Well, I'll tell
you this example.

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:39.180
So let's assume your
prompt is about Teddy

00:36:39.180 --> 00:36:40.840
who is in a sealed box.

00:36:40.840 --> 00:36:42.660
And you want to--
you're actually

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:44.080
moving the box to the kitchen.

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:47.260
And now you're asking
yourself where is Teddy?

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:50.460
And let's assume your
model says, OK, step 1,

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:52.360
Teddy climbs out of the box.

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:54.180
Step 2, teddy walks
to the kitchen.

00:36:54.180 --> 00:36:57.100
And step 3, answer-- kitchen.

00:36:57.100 --> 00:36:59.860
So know that the
answer is correct

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:04.830
because you move the
box to the kitchen.

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:07.270
But what is not
correct is Teddy who's

00:37:07.270 --> 00:37:12.830
working because Teddy is
in the box the whole time.

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:17.310
And in the vanilla
case, you would

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:19.950
incentivize all tokens
of this response

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:22.390
to happen more frequently.

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:24.910
And this is not
what you want to do.

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:30.750
So you're lacking an explicit
token level credit assignment.

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:34.230
That is something that
we need to work on.

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:36.750
And then the third
one I think we briefly

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:40.030
mentioned it last time.

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:42.710
The policy that you already
have at the beginning

00:37:42.710 --> 00:37:46.750
of this training stage is
already at a good state.

00:37:46.750 --> 00:37:48.770
You already did pretraining.

00:37:48.770 --> 00:37:49.930
You already did your SFT.

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:54.950
Your model is useful, and you
don't want your trained model

00:37:54.950 --> 00:37:58.150
to deviate too
much from the model

00:37:58.150 --> 00:38:02.230
that you trained because
it's already good.

00:38:02.230 --> 00:38:06.440
And on top of that, sometimes
the rewards that you have,

00:38:06.440 --> 00:38:10.840
they're not exactly
aligned to your end goal.

00:38:10.840 --> 00:38:13.120
So I'll give you the
following example.

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:20.600
So let's assume me I
come here, and in order

00:38:20.600 --> 00:38:24.940
to know if I did a good
job at giving this lecture,

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:27.840
I would rely on the
intensity of the claps

00:38:27.840 --> 00:38:30.520
that you would make at
the end of the lecture.

00:38:30.520 --> 00:38:34.840
So if I were to only
optimize for that,

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:37.680
then that would not be ideal,
because maybe I would just

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:40.320
spend an hour or two
hours making jokes that

00:38:40.320 --> 00:38:42.060
are not related to the class.

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:44.880
I would still
receive claps, maybe,

00:38:44.880 --> 00:38:48.560
but it would not be
aligned with my objective.

00:38:48.560 --> 00:38:52.320
And so there is this
risk of reward hacking,

00:38:52.320 --> 00:38:57.640
where your reward model,
may actually be imperfect.

00:38:57.640 --> 00:38:59.120
And you may have
your model who's

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:04.530
just trying to game the reward
model, and you don't want that.

00:39:04.530 --> 00:39:09.630
So I hope I convinced you
that we don't want the policy.

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:12.850
We don't want the model to
be too far from the model

00:39:12.850 --> 00:39:14.570
that you already have.

00:39:14.570 --> 00:39:21.290
So we want to have some sense
of constraints in there.

00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:23.530
And we will see how we can
quantify the difference

00:39:23.530 --> 00:39:24.670
between distribution.

00:39:24.670 --> 00:39:25.170
Yeah?

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:27.010
[INAUDIBLE]

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:28.770
So the question
is, what do we mean

00:39:28.770 --> 00:39:30.870
by don't want to regress
on previous tasks?

00:39:30.870 --> 00:39:33.810
So your model was trained
for specific things.

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:38.090
And you're now asking it to
be better at something else.

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:42.290
And so the capabilities
that it has acquired

00:39:42.290 --> 00:39:47.970
can be something that it loses
if you emphasize too much

00:39:47.970 --> 00:39:51.290
on the task of interest here.

00:39:51.290 --> 00:39:54.450
And so you also want your
model to not deviate too much

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:55.350
because of that.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:00.500
OK.

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:01.080
Cool.

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:02.510
Summary.

00:40:02.510 --> 00:40:03.760
What are the three challenges?

00:40:03.760 --> 00:40:07.140
The first one is
gradients can be noisy.

00:40:07.140 --> 00:40:11.540
Second one is we don't have
out-of-the-box an explicit

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:15.300
token-level credit assignment.

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:20.780
And the third one is we don't
have any explicit constraints

00:40:20.780 --> 00:40:24.460
on how far the policy can go
with respect to the policy

00:40:24.460 --> 00:40:27.380
that we're starting from.

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:30.620
So with these three
challenges in mind,

00:40:30.620 --> 00:40:36.140
what we will do is talk about
the most common RL algorithms

00:40:36.140 --> 00:40:40.820
that are out there, which
I'm sure you've heard of.

00:40:40.820 --> 00:40:45.580
We will start with
the first one, PPO.

00:40:45.580 --> 00:40:51.820
So PPO stands for Proximal
Policy Optimization.

00:40:51.820 --> 00:40:54.860
And what it tries
to do is it tries

00:40:54.860 --> 00:40:58.380
to maximize your
objective function

00:40:58.380 --> 00:41:03.580
but with two differences
compared to the first one.

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:07.740
The first difference is it
tries to maximize advantages

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:10.180
and not rewards.

00:41:10.180 --> 00:41:12.180
And the second
difference is it tries

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:18.020
to not deviate too much from
the policy that you had before.

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:22.140
And we will see exactly how
these two terms are obtained.

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:24.100
But just one note.

00:41:24.100 --> 00:41:28.060
PPO, the P in PPO is
for that second term.

00:41:28.060 --> 00:41:35.740
You want a bit of proximity
to that policy of reference.

00:41:35.740 --> 00:41:40.200
So with that being said,
let's go into the first term,

00:41:40.200 --> 00:41:43.580
which is the advantage term.

00:41:43.580 --> 00:41:46.260
So the reward
function that you have

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:50.300
is wondering how good is
this answer with respect

00:41:50.300 --> 00:41:51.980
to the prompt.

00:41:51.980 --> 00:41:55.220
But what we really
want to ask is,

00:41:55.220 --> 00:41:59.670
compared to what we would expect
for this prompt, how good is

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:02.030
this response?

00:42:02.030 --> 00:42:07.070
So we want to have some form
of relative to a baseline,

00:42:07.070 --> 00:42:12.350
and that is what
advantages are aiming for.

00:42:12.350 --> 00:42:17.550
And in order to illustrate that,
let's go through an example.

00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:20.470
So let's assume that we
have the following prompts.

00:42:20.470 --> 00:42:21.750
Teddy is in a box.

00:42:21.750 --> 00:42:23.230
Where it's Teddy?

00:42:23.230 --> 00:42:25.150
And then you have
your response--

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:27.630
in the box.

00:42:27.630 --> 00:42:30.190
So the reward for
that one-- let's

00:42:30.190 --> 00:42:31.650
assume it's a correct response.

00:42:31.650 --> 00:42:34.070
So the reward for
the whole completion

00:42:34.070 --> 00:42:37.990
relative to that prompt
is, let's say, plus 1.

00:42:37.990 --> 00:42:44.590
But you want to know how these
different tokens are actually

00:42:44.590 --> 00:42:50.790
receiving the credits relative
to what they are expecting.

00:42:50.790 --> 00:42:55.110
So the question that we
want to ask ourselves

00:42:55.110 --> 00:43:00.960
is, given a partial
completion, what

00:43:00.960 --> 00:43:05.080
is the expected
final reward if you

00:43:05.080 --> 00:43:07.800
were to continue
sampling from the policy

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:10.320
for the rest of the tokens?

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:12.520
So I'll give you an example.

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:15.280
So let's assume you
have the whole input.

00:43:15.280 --> 00:43:17.320
And then you're
decoding, let's say,

00:43:17.320 --> 00:43:21.320
in here you want to
ask yourself, OK,

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:25.120
if I were to continue like
this on average, what would

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:28.000
be the expected final rewards?

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:31.040
And here, let's
suppose it's 0.6.

00:43:31.040 --> 00:43:31.900
Then you decode.

00:43:31.900 --> 00:43:37.360
You go to the, OK, 0.7 box.

00:43:37.360 --> 00:43:38.500
It's the correct answer.

00:43:38.500 --> 00:43:44.440
So it should have a big bump,
so let's say 0.95 and 1.

00:43:44.440 --> 00:43:51.160
So what did we do here, what we
tried to do is to ask ourselves,

00:43:51.160 --> 00:43:56.730
given a partial completion, what
the expected final reward would

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:58.130
be.

00:43:58.130 --> 00:44:02.210
And the nice thing
with this approach

00:44:02.210 --> 00:44:04.870
is we have a sense
of a baseline.

00:44:04.870 --> 00:44:08.170
And the baseline
here is compared

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:13.050
to how we would have
completed if you

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:15.770
were to sample the policy.

00:44:15.770 --> 00:44:21.890
And you can exactly pinpoint the
contribution of a given token

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:23.730
when you do that.

00:44:23.730 --> 00:44:27.130
So in this example,
if you were to compute

00:44:27.130 --> 00:44:34.370
the very simple version of the
advantage for the token box,

00:44:34.370 --> 00:44:40.010
you would take
the value with box

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:42.770
and subtract the
value without box.

00:44:42.770 --> 00:44:47.230
So it's 0.95 minus
7, so plus 25.

00:44:50.010 --> 00:44:54.540
So I hope this example
motivates the fact

00:44:54.540 --> 00:45:00.020
that we're introducing a
new function, which here we

00:45:00.020 --> 00:45:03.380
call the value function.

00:45:03.380 --> 00:45:07.700
That is a function that is
defined at the token level.

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:11.140
So what that function
does, so the math

00:45:11.140 --> 00:45:13.180
formula on the
slide, what it says

00:45:13.180 --> 00:45:18.420
is it tries to determine
what is the expected

00:45:18.420 --> 00:45:22.100
future rewards if
you were to start

00:45:22.100 --> 00:45:27.100
from the input and
the partial completion

00:45:27.100 --> 00:45:31.340
and if you were to continue
decoding by sampling

00:45:31.340 --> 00:45:35.140
from your LLM, by sampling
from the policy, what would

00:45:35.140 --> 00:45:38.380
be the expected value of that?

00:45:38.380 --> 00:45:40.380
That is your baseline.

00:45:40.380 --> 00:45:44.940
So in this case, the
model is actually

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:50.500
trained along with the policy
because you need to ask yourself

00:45:50.500 --> 00:45:52.750
what would be the
expected reward if you

00:45:52.750 --> 00:45:55.150
were to sample from the policy?

00:45:55.150 --> 00:45:59.270
So you need to leverage the
parameters of the policy

00:45:59.270 --> 00:46:02.030
that you're training.

00:46:02.030 --> 00:46:04.110
Here, the way you would
train such a model

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:08.630
is you would try to
predict the reward

00:46:08.630 --> 00:46:13.230
at the end of the completion,
given a partial completion along

00:46:13.230 --> 00:46:16.110
with the prompt.

00:46:16.110 --> 00:46:19.630
And this quantity would be
defined at the token level.

00:46:19.630 --> 00:46:25.630
So for each token of the
output, you would have a value.

00:46:25.630 --> 00:46:28.950
So this is the value function.

00:46:28.950 --> 00:46:32.550
And the way you would
compute the advantage

00:46:32.550 --> 00:46:37.390
is by taking the
value up to that

00:46:37.390 --> 00:46:44.470
token and do minus the value
up to the token right before.

00:46:44.470 --> 00:46:47.790
And here you're
able to tease out

00:46:47.790 --> 00:46:53.000
the impact of that token itself.

00:46:53.000 --> 00:46:56.880
So one thing that
I want to say it

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:59.160
is actually more
complicated than that.

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:01.780
So this, that's
why it's in quotes.

00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:03.878
So it's a quote unquote.

00:47:03.878 --> 00:47:05.920
The reason why it's a
little bit more complicated

00:47:05.920 --> 00:47:11.400
than that is if you were
to generate a token,

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:15.660
you may increase the likelihood
of you getting a high reward,

00:47:15.660 --> 00:47:17.040
let's say.

00:47:17.040 --> 00:47:20.720
But you may also put
yourself in a position

00:47:20.720 --> 00:47:24.120
where future tokens would
benefit from the token

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:26.040
that you generated.

00:47:26.040 --> 00:47:29.920
So let me give you an example.

00:47:29.920 --> 00:47:33.880
Let's assume you're teaching
a concept to someone.

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:38.160
You're teaching it
some math concepts.

00:47:38.160 --> 00:47:43.360
You teaching the concepts
may lead to your friends

00:47:43.360 --> 00:47:45.440
acing the math exam.

00:47:45.440 --> 00:47:48.520
So that's a short-term reward.

00:47:48.520 --> 00:47:55.570
But you can also assume that the
fact of them succeeding the exam

00:47:55.570 --> 00:48:01.150
is also placing them on a good
path for succeeding later exams

00:48:01.150 --> 00:48:04.970
and maybe being
successful at work

00:48:04.970 --> 00:48:07.790
and then starting a
company, being a CEO,

00:48:07.790 --> 00:48:08.750
being a billionaire.

00:48:08.750 --> 00:48:11.810
So you cannot completely
attribute you teaching

00:48:11.810 --> 00:48:15.810
the concept to your friend as
being the sole reason for them

00:48:15.810 --> 00:48:17.270
being successful in the future.

00:48:17.270 --> 00:48:19.210
But there is some
part of it that

00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:22.250
should be attributed to that.

00:48:22.250 --> 00:48:27.210
And that is the idea behind the
generalized advantage estimation

00:48:27.210 --> 00:48:29.310
paper, which is not
part of the exam.

00:48:29.310 --> 00:48:31.910
It's not part of the
scope of this class.

00:48:31.910 --> 00:48:36.930
But in case you're interested,
feel free to look at it.

00:48:36.930 --> 00:48:43.010
The idea is to also look at
how future values also perform

00:48:43.010 --> 00:48:45.790
and link that back
to a given token.

00:48:50.100 --> 00:48:51.300
Cool.

00:48:51.300 --> 00:48:53.040
So, so far, does
that make sense?

00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:58.220
So here what we saw was a way
to compute advantages, which

00:48:58.220 --> 00:49:04.740
are a quantity that tells you
how a given token, predicted

00:49:04.740 --> 00:49:09.880
token is impacting your
purpose of getting, let's say,

00:49:09.880 --> 00:49:13.620
high rewards or getting
token level signal?

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:17.780
And you also have a
sense of a baseline

00:49:17.780 --> 00:49:22.680
by comparing your value with the
value without that given token.

00:49:22.680 --> 00:49:23.180
Yes?

00:49:23.180 --> 00:49:25.300
[INAUDIBLE]

00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:28.620
So the question is, is there any
interaction between the value

00:49:28.620 --> 00:49:30.040
function and the policy?

00:49:30.040 --> 00:49:33.500
So these two are
actually two models.

00:49:33.500 --> 00:49:37.100
In practice, you
train them jointly.

00:49:37.100 --> 00:49:39.620
So you would have
one loss function

00:49:39.620 --> 00:49:42.980
and have a penalization
for the value

00:49:42.980 --> 00:49:47.750
and the penalization
for the actual policy.

00:49:47.750 --> 00:49:50.890
But in this case, these two
are completely disjoint.

00:49:50.890 --> 00:49:53.870
The value function is not
being fed as input anywhere.

00:49:57.410 --> 00:49:57.910
Yeah?

00:49:57.910 --> 00:49:58.710
[INAUDIBLE]

00:49:58.710 --> 00:50:00.310
So the question is
the reward model

00:50:00.310 --> 00:50:03.130
and the value model
are both trained.

00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:05.230
So the reward model
is trained beforehand.

00:50:05.230 --> 00:50:08.510
And the value model is trained
at the same time as when

00:50:08.510 --> 00:50:10.390
you're training your policy.

00:50:10.390 --> 00:50:12.150
And the main reason
why you want to do

00:50:12.150 --> 00:50:17.310
that this way is because the
definition of your value model

00:50:17.310 --> 00:50:21.650
is to determine the
expected rewards

00:50:21.650 --> 00:50:24.590
if you were to continue
according to the policies

00:50:24.590 --> 00:50:25.570
that you're training.

00:50:30.310 --> 00:50:32.150
Cool.

00:50:32.150 --> 00:50:32.690
Yeah?

00:50:32.690 --> 00:50:34.550
[INAUDIBLE]

00:50:34.550 --> 00:50:37.810
So the question is value
function is a function of theta.

00:50:37.810 --> 00:50:39.970
So yes, there is a
backbone that is shared.

00:50:39.970 --> 00:50:44.120
And the gradients are determined
by your loss function, which

00:50:44.120 --> 00:50:47.960
contains a value loss
function and then

00:50:47.960 --> 00:50:52.707
a policy, the actual
loss function.

00:50:52.707 --> 00:50:54.540
So in case you're
interested in the details,

00:50:54.540 --> 00:50:58.260
actually the PPO paper does
have some technical details.

00:50:58.260 --> 00:51:01.420
They're interesting to look at.

00:51:04.440 --> 00:51:07.280
So now what we're
saying is we're not

00:51:07.280 --> 00:51:10.500
trying to optimize the
expected value of the rewards.

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:12.880
We're trying to optimize
the expected value

00:51:12.880 --> 00:51:15.160
of the advantages.

00:51:15.160 --> 00:51:18.800
Well, not exactly.

00:51:18.800 --> 00:51:23.000
So if you remember
this, so assuming you

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:29.440
can all see the
blackboard at the top, we

00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:35.320
that generating
completions were expensive.

00:51:35.320 --> 00:51:37.560
So do we want to generate
completions every time?

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:39.680
No.

00:51:39.680 --> 00:51:45.530
So what you want to do is
to leverage completions

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:47.570
for multiple updates.

00:51:47.570 --> 00:51:50.490
And here you can do
that by generating

00:51:50.490 --> 00:51:58.810
a batch using pi theta
and then update pi theta

00:51:58.810 --> 00:52:00.030
on that same batch.

00:52:00.030 --> 00:52:01.810
And the pi theta
would become pi old

00:52:01.810 --> 00:52:06.450
because it's not the
current policy anymore.

00:52:06.450 --> 00:52:09.810
And so that is the
reason why you would see

00:52:09.810 --> 00:52:13.170
in papers that ratio pop up.

00:52:13.170 --> 00:52:16.830
So you're not optimizing for
expectation of the advantage.

00:52:16.830 --> 00:52:19.970
You're optimizing for the
expectation of the ratio

00:52:19.970 --> 00:52:21.770
times the advantage.

00:52:21.770 --> 00:52:25.970
And this is because you
want to adjust for the fact

00:52:25.970 --> 00:52:28.790
that you're actually
sampling from the old policy.

00:52:28.790 --> 00:52:30.610
But you want to
make sure that this

00:52:30.610 --> 00:52:33.170
is corresponding to
the current policy.

00:52:33.170 --> 00:52:36.470
So you have this
corrective term,

00:52:36.470 --> 00:52:39.950
which is the ratio between the
current and the old policy.

00:52:43.090 --> 00:52:45.870
And this is where I want
to give a disclaimer.

00:52:45.870 --> 00:52:48.170
So some papers they
note this ratio

00:52:48.170 --> 00:52:51.430
R. So this can be confusing
with respect to the rewards.

00:52:51.430 --> 00:52:56.890
So here we note this rho,
and it is called importance

00:52:56.890 --> 00:52:59.770
sampling ratio.

00:52:59.770 --> 00:53:01.290
You have a question?

00:53:01.290 --> 00:53:02.310
No?

00:53:02.310 --> 00:53:02.810
OK.

00:53:02.810 --> 00:53:04.130
Great.

00:53:04.130 --> 00:53:09.610
So what can happen if
you do things this way?

00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:14.530
Well, you can run into the issue
that we discussed previously

00:53:14.530 --> 00:53:18.770
where you may run
into a situation

00:53:18.770 --> 00:53:22.610
where you're updating your
theta such that it deviates too

00:53:22.610 --> 00:53:25.770
much from previous policies.

00:53:25.770 --> 00:53:27.810
So what you actually
want to do is

00:53:27.810 --> 00:53:33.650
to have some form of a
control over that ratio.

00:53:33.650 --> 00:53:39.090
And you don't want that ratio to
be, let's say, too far from one.

00:53:39.090 --> 00:53:41.140
So there is a formula for that.

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:44.100
And I don't want you to look
at the formula right now.

00:53:44.100 --> 00:53:47.180
I want us to just
take a look at it

00:53:47.180 --> 00:53:50.700
from an intuitive perspective.

00:53:50.700 --> 00:53:54.820
So here is what I want us to do.

00:53:54.820 --> 00:53:59.860
So let's assume that
we're interested in how

00:53:59.860 --> 00:54:05.420
much your model is
updating the policy.

00:54:05.420 --> 00:54:10.780
So here we said it's
something like the ratio

00:54:10.780 --> 00:54:13.940
times the advantage.

00:54:13.940 --> 00:54:18.100
So it's linear as a
function of the advantage.

00:54:18.100 --> 00:54:21.340
So if you were to draw--

00:54:21.340 --> 00:54:26.140
I'm going to just replicate
what we have here--

00:54:26.140 --> 00:54:31.940
the evolution of this objective
function, which I will note J,

00:54:31.940 --> 00:54:36.140
as a function of the ratio--

00:54:36.140 --> 00:54:39.250
so if A is positive, so if
your advantage is positive,

00:54:39.250 --> 00:54:41.870
meaning you want your
model to increase

00:54:41.870 --> 00:54:46.230
the probability of a given
action from occurring,

00:54:46.230 --> 00:54:49.670
then you will have
something like this.

00:54:49.670 --> 00:54:53.270
Like, A is positive.

00:54:53.270 --> 00:54:56.230
Does everyone agree with me?

00:54:56.230 --> 00:54:59.130
So let's assume here we have 1.

00:55:01.770 --> 00:55:03.290
So I'm just going to recap.

00:55:03.290 --> 00:55:09.390
So rho is pi theta over pi old.

00:55:09.390 --> 00:55:15.550
So 1 means that your current
policy and the old policy

00:55:15.550 --> 00:55:18.070
are saying the same thing.

00:55:18.070 --> 00:55:21.390
And so if you're
here, then what you

00:55:21.390 --> 00:55:27.790
want to do in order to perform
your update is to maximize J.

00:55:27.790 --> 00:55:29.110
So how do you maximize J?

00:55:29.110 --> 00:55:35.830
You maximize J by increasing
the probability of your policy

00:55:35.830 --> 00:55:37.090
performing that action.

00:55:37.090 --> 00:55:41.560
So you would go more
somewhere there.

00:55:41.560 --> 00:55:46.800
You would have your new policy
be updated and correspond here.

00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:52.040
The problem is, if you don't
do anything to that expression,

00:55:52.040 --> 00:55:56.920
the gradient is always
nonzero and incentivizing

00:55:56.920 --> 00:56:00.800
you to increase rho to
increase the probability

00:56:00.800 --> 00:56:06.160
of your policy generating
that action more and more.

00:56:06.160 --> 00:56:14.960
So what you want to do is to
break that incentive and clip

00:56:14.960 --> 00:56:19.480
that function in order for
you to have a 0 gradient

00:56:19.480 --> 00:56:22.740
region where if you
were to, let's say,

00:56:22.740 --> 00:56:26.960
perform an update to your
policy and it would become here,

00:56:26.960 --> 00:56:32.680
you would have no
incentive to increase rho.

00:56:32.680 --> 00:56:36.970
And this is exactly
what is on the slides.

00:56:36.970 --> 00:56:40.490
What we want to do is
to clip that function,

00:56:40.490 --> 00:56:45.250
to remove the incentive
from the update

00:56:45.250 --> 00:56:50.650
to increase the probability
of an action being generated.

00:56:50.650 --> 00:56:55.410
And you want to avoid your
policy from increasing

00:56:55.410 --> 00:56:57.370
that probability even more.

00:56:57.370 --> 00:56:59.370
So in other words, you
don't want your policy

00:56:59.370 --> 00:57:02.650
to deviate too much
from the old policy.

00:57:02.650 --> 00:57:05.690
So this is when the
advantage is positive.

00:57:05.690 --> 00:57:09.890
Similarly, very similar
fashion when it's negative.

00:57:09.890 --> 00:57:15.930
Here, the objective function
will incentivize your model

00:57:15.930 --> 00:57:18.970
to decrease your ratio, to
decrease the probability

00:57:18.970 --> 00:57:23.770
that your model has from
generating an action that

00:57:23.770 --> 00:57:26.050
leads to negative advantage.

00:57:26.050 --> 00:57:28.370
So you will have
something like this.

00:57:28.370 --> 00:57:30.510
So if you come back
to the formula,

00:57:30.510 --> 00:57:33.270
this is what the
formula is doing.

00:57:33.270 --> 00:57:33.770
Yes?

00:57:33.770 --> 00:57:36.820
[INAUDIBLE]

00:57:36.820 --> 00:57:40.720
So the question is you had
introduced the KL divergence.

00:57:40.720 --> 00:57:41.800
Why don't you use that?

00:57:41.800 --> 00:57:43.580
Well, it's my next slide.

00:57:43.580 --> 00:57:46.160
So I will say a little
bit about how that works.

00:57:46.160 --> 00:57:50.540
But this is one way of
constraining your model

00:57:50.540 --> 00:57:53.720
from being updated too much.

00:57:53.720 --> 00:57:55.720
So to your point, let's
go to the KL divergence.

00:57:55.720 --> 00:57:58.780
So what is the KL
divergence doing?

00:57:58.780 --> 00:58:02.020
So it is an operator that
is taking in two probability

00:58:02.020 --> 00:58:06.340
distributions, let's
assume p and q.

00:58:06.340 --> 00:58:13.300
So here this operator is
assuming that p is true,

00:58:13.300 --> 00:58:16.460
and it is weighting
that by log of pi/q.

00:58:19.380 --> 00:58:22.020
You have also the
continuous version, which

00:58:22.020 --> 00:58:23.480
is just take the integral.

00:58:26.060 --> 00:58:29.860
So you have a second way
of quantifying deviations

00:58:29.860 --> 00:58:35.590
of your model by saying you
actually want a penalization

00:58:35.590 --> 00:58:42.710
term in your loss function that
compares the current policy

00:58:42.710 --> 00:58:45.950
and some other policy.

00:58:45.950 --> 00:58:51.270
So here typically what
people do is they actually

00:58:51.270 --> 00:58:53.350
penalize your current
policy from being

00:58:53.350 --> 00:58:56.830
too different from your
SFT policy, from your base

00:58:56.830 --> 00:58:59.590
policy, your reference
policy, which

00:58:59.590 --> 00:59:01.110
is why I want you
to pay attention

00:59:01.110 --> 00:59:05.030
to the subscripts of every
pi that you see out there.

00:59:05.030 --> 00:59:08.430
So here it's pi theta
with respect to pi ref.

00:59:08.430 --> 00:59:12.550
Pi ref is reference,
as in the base policy.

00:59:12.550 --> 00:59:16.230
And this contrasts
with pi old, which

00:59:16.230 --> 00:59:22.070
is the policy that you use
to generate the completions.

00:59:22.070 --> 00:59:24.870
So I'll give you a full story.

00:59:24.870 --> 00:59:27.830
You start with your pi ref.

00:59:27.830 --> 00:59:31.200
You train your model,
so you generate a batch.

00:59:31.200 --> 00:59:32.540
This becomes pi old.

00:59:32.540 --> 00:59:36.340
You perform several updates, and
then you generate another batch.

00:59:36.340 --> 00:59:39.840
This becomes your pi
old, several updates.

00:59:39.840 --> 00:59:45.280
Pi ref stays fixed, and this
is what you're comparing

00:59:45.280 --> 00:59:48.920
your current policy to.

00:59:48.920 --> 00:59:54.680
And so I want to
very quickly mention

00:59:54.680 --> 00:59:57.700
a scary formula
we saw last week,

00:59:57.700 --> 01:00:00.640
which was the one from DPO.

01:00:00.640 --> 01:00:03.460
And I'm not sure
if you remember,

01:00:03.460 --> 01:00:09.360
but we had said that
there was a supervised way

01:00:09.360 --> 01:00:15.200
to express the RL
objective by using

01:00:15.200 --> 01:00:18.680
the Bradley-Terry formulation.

01:00:18.680 --> 01:00:23.280
And what we said was that the
objective function was following

01:00:23.280 --> 01:00:26.000
something that was very
much into the line of what

01:00:26.000 --> 01:00:29.280
the Bradley-Terry was saying,
which is something like,

01:00:29.280 --> 01:00:39.970
so this one is like L minus
expectation of log of sigma.

01:00:39.970 --> 01:00:44.850
And sigma is your sigmoidal
which is sigma of, let's say,

01:00:44.850 --> 01:00:48.050
Rw minus Rl.

01:00:48.050 --> 01:00:52.290
And we had said
that R was defined

01:00:52.290 --> 01:01:03.130
as beta log of your policy
compared to some other policy.

01:01:03.130 --> 01:01:05.690
And here let's say
it's a reference.

01:01:05.690 --> 01:01:11.450
And we had said that beta was
a quantity, some quantity.

01:01:11.450 --> 01:01:14.850
So I just want to
draw a connection here

01:01:14.850 --> 01:01:17.730
that the beta that
you're seeing in the KL

01:01:17.730 --> 01:01:21.000
divergence, the beta that
is actually penalizing

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:23.250
for your model being too
different from your reference

01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:27.330
model, is actually
the same beta.

01:01:27.330 --> 01:01:31.300
So the higher the
beta, the more you

01:01:31.300 --> 01:01:36.620
will try to not deviate too
much from your reference policy.

01:01:36.620 --> 01:01:38.540
And I just want to
show you mathematically

01:01:38.540 --> 01:01:41.540
why that is the
case intuitively.

01:01:41.540 --> 01:01:48.180
So sigma, if you were
to draw the function,

01:01:48.180 --> 01:01:50.300
it's something like this.

01:01:50.300 --> 01:01:51.220
Whoop.

01:01:51.220 --> 01:01:52.780
Like this.

01:01:52.780 --> 01:01:55.980
It's like 1 and 0.

01:01:55.980 --> 01:02:04.120
So sigma of x is 1 over 1
plus exponential of minus x.

01:02:04.120 --> 01:02:08.380
So when x is 0, it's 0.5.

01:02:08.380 --> 01:02:11.460
If x goes to plus
infinity, this goes to 1.

01:02:11.460 --> 01:02:17.060
And when x goes to minus
infinity, this goes to 0.

01:02:17.060 --> 01:02:25.140
So there is a region where the
sigmoid evolves very quickly.

01:02:25.140 --> 01:02:30.670
And so we had said that
the incentive of your loss

01:02:30.670 --> 01:02:37.430
is for this difference
to be as big as possible.

01:02:37.430 --> 01:02:39.590
And how do you do that?

01:02:39.590 --> 01:02:42.250
You want to do Rw
as big as possible.

01:02:42.250 --> 01:02:43.510
And how do you do that?

01:02:43.510 --> 01:02:47.030
You do that by increasing the
probability of your policy

01:02:47.030 --> 01:02:51.350
generating the winning answer.

01:02:51.350 --> 01:02:58.750
But if the better that
you have is a big beta,

01:02:58.750 --> 01:03:02.430
then that means that
even a slight deviation

01:03:02.430 --> 01:03:10.150
from the reference policy will
make this whole value big.

01:03:10.150 --> 01:03:13.150
And you will already
be in a region

01:03:13.150 --> 01:03:14.690
where you're already close to 1.

01:03:17.830 --> 01:03:22.790
So in other words, if
you have a big beta,

01:03:22.790 --> 01:03:25.830
your policy will have
a lower incentive

01:03:25.830 --> 01:03:29.470
to be far from the
reference policy.

01:03:29.470 --> 01:03:32.190
And this contrasts
with the lower beta.

01:03:32.190 --> 01:03:34.390
So if you have a
low beta, then you

01:03:34.390 --> 01:03:38.430
need this quantity to be much
higher than this quantity

01:03:38.430 --> 01:03:40.290
for this to be big.

01:03:43.190 --> 01:03:46.010
So I'm not sure if that helps.

01:03:46.010 --> 01:03:50.590
But all that to say,
that beta is actually

01:03:50.590 --> 01:03:56.910
linked to your incentive
to want to make

01:03:56.910 --> 01:03:59.450
this close to the
reference policy or not.

01:03:59.450 --> 01:04:02.130
And this is the same beta
that is in the DPO loss.

01:04:02.130 --> 01:04:02.630
Yes?

01:04:02.630 --> 01:04:05.110
[INAUDIBLE]

01:04:05.110 --> 01:04:07.810
So the question is rho
t, is it using pi old?

01:04:07.810 --> 01:04:09.510
Typically, yes.

01:04:09.510 --> 01:04:11.210
And here it is pi ref.

01:04:11.210 --> 01:04:11.710
Yes?

01:04:11.710 --> 01:04:12.697
[INAUDIBLE]

01:04:12.697 --> 01:04:14.530
So just to confirm there
are three policies.

01:04:14.530 --> 01:04:15.910
Yes, there are three policies.

01:04:15.910 --> 01:04:19.110
So pi theta is the current
policy that you're updating.

01:04:19.110 --> 01:04:22.610
Pi old is the one that you
use to generate the batch.

01:04:22.610 --> 01:04:25.520
And then pi ref is
your base model.

01:04:25.520 --> 01:04:26.820
Yes, was it the same question?

01:04:26.820 --> 01:04:28.400
[INAUDIBLE]

01:04:28.400 --> 01:04:30.800
Yeah, so the question
is, if you only

01:04:30.800 --> 01:04:34.360
care about how a policy
deviates from its old value,

01:04:34.360 --> 01:04:36.360
how do you make sure that
it doesn't deviate too

01:04:36.360 --> 01:04:38.300
much from the reference one?

01:04:38.300 --> 01:04:40.960
Typically what people do
is they have these two

01:04:40.960 --> 01:04:43.400
constraints simultaneously.

01:04:43.400 --> 01:04:46.080
They have the clipping
of the ratio with respect

01:04:46.080 --> 01:04:49.480
to the old policy,
so the iteration

01:04:49.480 --> 01:04:54.800
constraints, along with
the absolute respect

01:04:54.800 --> 01:04:57.840
to the reference policy one.

01:04:57.840 --> 01:05:02.680
So they're both penalizing for
deviation with the old policy

01:05:02.680 --> 01:05:05.860
but also with respect to
the absolute base policy.

01:05:05.860 --> 01:05:08.540
[INAUDIBLE]

01:05:08.540 --> 01:05:09.040
Yeah?

01:05:09.040 --> 01:05:10.680
[INAUDIBLE]

01:05:10.680 --> 01:05:12.398
The question is,
how you compute KL?

01:05:12.398 --> 01:05:13.440
So it's a great question.

01:05:13.440 --> 01:05:16.140
Because KL is expensive because
it's over the whole vocabulary,

01:05:16.140 --> 01:05:17.920
you have some
estimator for that.

01:05:17.920 --> 01:05:20.388
Yeah.

01:05:20.388 --> 01:05:21.180
You had a question?

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:22.030
No.

01:05:22.030 --> 01:05:22.530
OK.

01:05:22.530 --> 01:05:23.850
Great.

01:05:23.850 --> 01:05:26.090
Let's move on.

01:05:26.090 --> 01:05:28.750
So if I were to
recap what we saw,

01:05:28.750 --> 01:05:33.850
we saw a way to maximize the
quantity that is actually

01:05:33.850 --> 01:05:36.330
as a function of, let's
say, a baseline, which

01:05:36.330 --> 01:05:37.570
is called advantage.

01:05:37.570 --> 01:05:41.370
And here we used the
value function to do that.

01:05:41.370 --> 01:05:46.810
And we also mentioned that
we had some things in place

01:05:46.810 --> 01:05:48.770
in order for us to
not deviate too much

01:05:48.770 --> 01:05:50.450
from what we had before.

01:05:50.450 --> 01:05:55.770
So we saw the clipping mechanism
with respect to the old policy,

01:05:55.770 --> 01:06:00.530
but we also saw the KL penalty
term between the current policy

01:06:00.530 --> 01:06:02.690
and the reference policy.

01:06:02.690 --> 01:06:04.810
So these are ideas.

01:06:04.810 --> 01:06:07.570
But some methods they
mix both, some methods

01:06:07.570 --> 01:06:09.230
they use one but not the other.

01:06:09.230 --> 01:06:12.370
Some methods mix them.

01:06:12.370 --> 01:06:13.868
There are multiple
flavors of it.

01:06:13.868 --> 01:06:15.410
But I think what is
important for you

01:06:15.410 --> 01:06:18.050
to remember is the
different methods

01:06:18.050 --> 01:06:23.740
that you can use in order
to achieve these goals.

01:06:23.740 --> 01:06:26.220
And I wanted to have
a dedicated slide

01:06:26.220 --> 01:06:30.740
to say if you were to read the
PPO paper, please be careful.

01:06:30.740 --> 01:06:32.913
L is not a loss.

01:06:32.913 --> 01:06:34.080
It is an objective function.

01:06:34.080 --> 01:06:36.860
You want to maximize
that quantity.

01:06:36.860 --> 01:06:38.420
You don't want to minimize.

01:06:38.420 --> 01:06:41.960
And the second one is you
will see R for the ratio.

01:06:41.960 --> 01:06:42.560
Be careful.

01:06:42.560 --> 01:06:43.560
It is not a reward.

01:06:43.560 --> 01:06:45.740
It is a ratio.

01:06:45.740 --> 01:06:48.180
And with that, I
will just recap what

01:06:48.180 --> 01:06:52.220
we saw with respect to the
challenges that we mentioned.

01:06:52.220 --> 01:06:55.740
Gradients being noisy,
well, we have a way

01:06:55.740 --> 01:07:01.420
to compute a baseline,
which also turns out to give

01:07:01.420 --> 01:07:03.940
token-level credit assignments.

01:07:03.940 --> 01:07:08.300
And we said we could also
use either clipping or the KL

01:07:08.300 --> 01:07:11.980
divergence methods in order to
ensure that the policy is not

01:07:11.980 --> 01:07:16.580
deviating too much
from what we had.

01:07:16.580 --> 01:07:19.790
But the big problem
with this approach

01:07:19.790 --> 01:07:25.990
is that you need to
train a value function.

01:07:25.990 --> 01:07:28.710
That is a lot of hassle.

01:07:28.710 --> 01:07:32.550
And so this is the reason why
this is not the only method

01:07:32.550 --> 01:07:34.470
we're seeing in, this lecture.

01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:37.830
There's a second method, which
has gained a lot of importance

01:07:37.830 --> 01:07:42.790
in the past couple of
years, which is called GRPO.

01:07:42.790 --> 01:07:48.630
So GRPO stands for Group
Relative Policy Optimization.

01:07:48.630 --> 01:07:54.510
And what it does is it
tries to maximize advantages

01:07:54.510 --> 01:07:58.630
but without a value function.

01:07:58.630 --> 01:08:02.230
That is the big added value.

01:08:02.230 --> 01:08:05.110
But without the value
function of GRPO

01:08:05.110 --> 01:08:09.790
is it doesn't need a value
function in order to work,

01:08:09.790 --> 01:08:13.670
and we're going to see
exactly what it does instead.

01:08:13.670 --> 01:08:17.149
So back to the
intuition that we had

01:08:17.149 --> 01:08:23.720
around having something
to compare a reward to,

01:08:23.720 --> 01:08:28.720
the intuition behind
GRPO is given a prompt,

01:08:28.720 --> 01:08:31.960
you're going to sample
multiple responses

01:08:31.960 --> 01:08:36.200
in order to get
that expected value.

01:08:36.200 --> 01:08:37.460
So you take some prompt.

01:08:37.460 --> 01:08:40.340
You take a positive temperature.

01:08:40.340 --> 01:08:42.380
And when you generate
several responses,

01:08:42.380 --> 01:08:44.279
they hopefully
are all different.

01:08:44.279 --> 01:08:47.760
And you will get some responses
that are better than others.

01:08:47.760 --> 01:08:51.800
And if you do that,
and if you compute

01:08:51.800 --> 01:08:54.800
the reward for each of
these outputs with respect

01:08:54.800 --> 01:08:59.040
to the prompts, then
if you compare that

01:08:59.040 --> 01:09:02.279
to the mean of reward
and you standardize them,

01:09:02.279 --> 01:09:07.920
you can get a sense of which
response is better to other one

01:09:07.920 --> 01:09:09.800
with respect to other ones.

01:09:09.800 --> 01:09:13.760
And you don't need a
value function for that.

01:09:13.760 --> 01:09:17.970
And of course, you also
want to not deviate

01:09:17.970 --> 01:09:20.250
too much from what
you had before.

01:09:20.250 --> 01:09:21.910
So the same tricks apply.

01:09:21.910 --> 01:09:27.529
So you have the clipping
mechanism which is making sure

01:09:27.529 --> 01:09:29.890
that you don't have
nonzero gradients if you're

01:09:29.890 --> 01:09:32.609
too far from the old policy.

01:09:32.609 --> 01:09:35.350
And you also have that
KL divergence term,

01:09:35.350 --> 01:09:39.410
which allows you to penalize any
deviations from the reference

01:09:39.410 --> 01:09:42.010
model.

01:09:42.010 --> 01:09:45.069
So it's a lot of math,
a lot of formulas.

01:09:45.069 --> 01:09:49.130
So let's relax for a second
and just walk through

01:09:49.130 --> 01:09:51.689
that illustration together.

01:09:51.689 --> 01:09:57.850
So how would we go about
updating the weights of our LLM

01:09:57.850 --> 01:10:01.890
if we were to follow GRPO?

01:10:01.890 --> 01:10:07.610
So first, we take
a prompt and ask

01:10:07.610 --> 01:10:12.970
our LLM, which is the policy
model, to generate responses.

01:10:12.970 --> 01:10:14.790
So it would generate one output.

01:10:14.790 --> 01:10:19.420
It will generate two
outputs, actually g outputs.

01:10:19.420 --> 01:10:24.820
And what you want is to know
how good these outputs are.

01:10:24.820 --> 01:10:27.620
So each of these outputs
with respect to the input

01:10:27.620 --> 01:10:31.660
would be evaluated with
respect to your reward

01:10:31.660 --> 01:10:37.380
model, which can be a model
or something deterministic.

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:41.980
And then you would
compute advantage values

01:10:41.980 --> 01:10:45.500
by subtracting the reward
with the mean of the rewards

01:10:45.500 --> 01:10:49.300
of that group and dividing this
with the standard deviation

01:10:49.300 --> 01:10:51.620
of the rewards.

01:10:51.620 --> 01:10:58.300
So here you get a relative sense
of how each output performs

01:10:58.300 --> 01:11:01.260
with respect to the group.

01:11:01.260 --> 01:11:03.420
And what you would
do is you would

01:11:03.420 --> 01:11:11.860
use these advantages in order
to compute your gradient.

01:11:11.860 --> 01:11:14.150
But you would also
make sure to penalize

01:11:14.150 --> 01:11:17.590
for any deviations with respect
to your reference model.

01:11:17.590 --> 01:11:19.350
So you would also
have a KL divergence

01:11:19.350 --> 01:11:23.350
term along with the clipping.

01:11:23.350 --> 01:11:26.190
And you would not have
any value function.

01:11:26.190 --> 01:11:27.690
No value function needed.

01:11:27.690 --> 01:11:29.930
You only need your policy model.

01:11:29.930 --> 01:11:32.968
You need the reward model
and a reference model.

01:11:32.968 --> 01:11:35.010
And by the way, the reward
model is optional too.

01:11:35.010 --> 01:11:37.710
Because if you're
doing, let's say,

01:11:37.710 --> 01:11:43.770
reasoning or math or coding, you
can leverage verifiable rewards.

01:11:43.770 --> 01:11:44.270
OK.

01:11:44.270 --> 01:11:48.790
So how does that
compare with PPO?

01:11:48.790 --> 01:11:53.190
So PPO, you took some inputs.

01:11:53.190 --> 01:11:54.930
You put that into
your policy model.

01:11:54.930 --> 01:11:57.630
You generate one response.

01:11:57.630 --> 01:12:01.710
And what you do is
you input that prompt

01:12:01.710 --> 01:12:03.870
and response into
a reward model,

01:12:03.870 --> 01:12:07.790
tells you how good
how well you're doing.

01:12:07.790 --> 01:12:14.680
There are many variations of how
to incorporate the KL penalty.

01:12:14.680 --> 01:12:18.160
But one of the variations
is to incorporate

01:12:18.160 --> 01:12:22.040
as part of the rewards.

01:12:22.040 --> 01:12:24.440
So you would do that.

01:12:24.440 --> 01:12:29.880
And in parallel to that, you
would use your value model

01:12:29.880 --> 01:12:34.680
in order to get a token-level
prediction of what

01:12:34.680 --> 01:12:39.640
is the expected final reward if
you were to follow that policy.

01:12:39.640 --> 01:12:45.240
And you would compute an
advantage quantity, which

01:12:45.240 --> 01:12:49.100
we saw is if we want to
be extremely vanilla,

01:12:49.100 --> 01:12:52.145
it can be the difference
between the value functions.

01:12:52.145 --> 01:12:54.020
But it's actually more
complicated than that.

01:12:54.020 --> 01:12:56.360
And you have a method
that does that that

01:12:56.360 --> 01:12:59.600
is called the Generalized
Advantage Estimation, that

01:12:59.600 --> 01:13:02.280
is called GAE.

01:13:02.280 --> 01:13:04.480
So you can do that.

01:13:04.480 --> 01:13:08.760
And then take your
advantage and use that

01:13:08.760 --> 01:13:15.130
in order to compute the gradient
that will update your policy.

01:13:15.130 --> 01:13:17.358
So I want us to--

01:13:17.358 --> 01:13:19.150
so by the way, does
that make sense so far?

01:13:22.890 --> 01:13:23.890
Yeah?

01:13:23.890 --> 01:13:26.050
OK.

01:13:26.050 --> 01:13:28.490
So with that being
said, I just want

01:13:28.490 --> 01:13:31.090
to do a recap of what are
the models that are involved

01:13:31.090 --> 01:13:34.810
and which ones are the ones
that we're training versus not.

01:13:34.810 --> 01:13:37.630
So if you remember, the
reference model is fixed.

01:13:37.630 --> 01:13:40.130
So it's the one that
you started from.

01:13:40.130 --> 01:13:43.870
And you have optionally
a reward model,

01:13:43.870 --> 01:13:47.890
which you train beforehand and
that you're leveraging here.

01:13:47.890 --> 01:13:50.850
You're not training
your reward model.

01:13:50.850 --> 01:13:55.570
But what you're training is
obviously your policy model.

01:13:55.570 --> 01:14:01.810
And in the case of PPO, you're
also training a value model.

01:14:01.810 --> 01:14:05.330
And you're training it
jointly with the policy model.

01:14:05.330 --> 01:14:08.010
Because as we saw,
the value model

01:14:08.010 --> 01:14:11.340
tries to estimate the
expected rewards if you

01:14:11.340 --> 01:14:14.012
were to follow the policy.

01:14:14.012 --> 01:14:15.720
And you are actually
training the policy.

01:14:15.720 --> 01:14:19.380
So you need to make sure
that these two are in sync.

01:14:19.380 --> 01:14:23.140
And that is the core reason why
PPO is a hassle, because you

01:14:23.140 --> 01:14:29.540
need to have all the bandwidth
to host the value model

01:14:29.540 --> 01:14:30.160
training.

01:14:32.900 --> 01:14:34.920
And at the end, you
obtain these formulas.

01:14:34.920 --> 01:14:37.720
And I'm glad we didn't
start from these formulas,

01:14:37.720 --> 01:14:41.340
because they can be a
little bit intimidating.

01:14:41.340 --> 01:14:44.020
So something that is
similar between the two

01:14:44.020 --> 01:14:46.380
formulas, the ratios.

01:14:46.380 --> 01:14:49.300
So here we have the ratio
between the probability given

01:14:49.300 --> 01:14:53.380
by the policy that
we're training over

01:14:53.380 --> 01:14:56.780
the probability of the policy
that is the one that generated

01:14:56.780 --> 01:14:59.340
the batch, so the old policy.

01:14:59.340 --> 01:15:05.380
That one is the quantity that
you see across both formulas.

01:15:05.380 --> 01:15:07.340
You also see the clipping.

01:15:07.340 --> 01:15:09.460
And here we saw
that the clipping

01:15:09.460 --> 01:15:15.380
was meant for us to make sure
that our policy is not going

01:15:15.380 --> 01:15:17.520
too far from the old policy.

01:15:20.220 --> 01:15:23.460
And then we also have
a KL divergence term.

01:15:23.460 --> 01:15:26.140
So I mentioned that there
are many variations.

01:15:26.140 --> 01:15:30.540
In here, GRPO actually
expresses the KL divergence

01:15:30.540 --> 01:15:32.620
in the loss function.

01:15:32.620 --> 01:15:35.580
But you can very well express
it as part of the advantage

01:15:35.580 --> 01:15:39.840
that you're computing,
which is the case of PPO.

01:15:43.340 --> 01:15:48.020
In terms of the differences, so
the previous thing that we saw

01:15:48.020 --> 01:15:49.500
was one difference, by the way.

01:15:49.500 --> 01:15:52.460
So where you put the KL
penalty is something that

01:15:52.460 --> 01:15:55.980
can vary from method to method.

01:15:55.980 --> 01:16:01.740
The second difference is the
way you compute the advantage.

01:16:01.740 --> 01:16:06.470
So in the GRPO case, what you
do is you compute the advantage

01:16:06.470 --> 01:16:10.310
at the completion
level, and you compare

01:16:10.310 --> 01:16:13.910
that with the other outputs.

01:16:13.910 --> 01:16:16.870
And so by the way, if you
look at the GRPO formula,

01:16:16.870 --> 01:16:21.710
you will see that T
denotes the token.

01:16:21.710 --> 01:16:25.950
And you see that the advantage
is a function of the output

01:16:25.950 --> 01:16:28.950
I and the token T. And you
may wonder, well why is there

01:16:28.950 --> 01:16:31.310
a T if it's at the
completion level?

01:16:31.310 --> 01:16:33.870
You can define the
advantage either

01:16:33.870 --> 01:16:37.710
at the completion level or the
token level, which is why there

01:16:37.710 --> 01:16:39.170
is this more generic formula.

01:16:39.170 --> 01:16:42.670
But people typically use
the completion level 1

01:16:42.670 --> 01:16:45.070
just because it's easier,
because the token level

01:16:45.070 --> 01:16:49.470
1 you need to have some way
of distinguishing token level

01:16:49.470 --> 01:16:53.830
contributions, which
you're typically not doing.

01:16:53.830 --> 01:16:56.590
So anyway, going
back, you see I and T,

01:16:56.590 --> 01:16:59.570
but it's actually a
function of I for GRPO.

01:16:59.570 --> 01:17:03.470
So you compute that by comparing
your reward with respect

01:17:03.470 --> 01:17:06.840
to the mean of the
rewards of your group,

01:17:06.840 --> 01:17:09.100
and you divide that by
the standard deviation.

01:17:09.100 --> 01:17:12.440
But for PPO, you're
computing the advantage

01:17:12.440 --> 01:17:15.880
at the token level,
and you're using

01:17:15.880 --> 01:17:17.960
the value function for that.

01:17:17.960 --> 01:17:19.680
And as a reminder,
the value function

01:17:19.680 --> 01:17:23.880
is also at the token level,
and it's trained jointly

01:17:23.880 --> 01:17:24.820
with the policy.

01:17:28.200 --> 01:17:29.360
And that's a lot.

01:17:29.360 --> 01:17:32.640
So I'll just pause
for maybe one minute

01:17:32.640 --> 01:17:37.400
to see if there is any questions
on these two big formulas.

01:17:37.400 --> 01:17:39.540
Now if you see this slide,
does that make sense?

01:17:43.760 --> 01:17:45.760
Yeah?

01:17:45.760 --> 01:17:46.260
Yeah?

01:17:46.260 --> 01:17:48.080
[INAUDIBLE]

01:17:48.080 --> 01:17:50.840
So the question is you
added in your previous slide

01:17:50.840 --> 01:17:52.260
the KL divergence for PPO.

01:17:52.260 --> 01:17:56.840
Yes, I did, but
these were more meant

01:17:56.840 --> 01:18:01.640
to illustrate two types
of strategies to penalize.

01:18:01.640 --> 01:18:04.848
But in the general
PPO implementation,

01:18:04.848 --> 01:18:07.390
you would actually incorporate
that as part of the advantage.

01:18:11.010 --> 01:18:13.570
And by the way,
it's a common thing.

01:18:13.570 --> 01:18:16.430
Every paper has a different
way of implementing this.

01:18:16.430 --> 01:18:21.590
So don't be surprised.

01:18:21.590 --> 01:18:22.090
OK.

01:18:22.090 --> 01:18:23.330
Cool.

01:18:23.330 --> 01:18:25.710
Well, everyone is good
with these big formulas.

01:18:25.710 --> 01:18:26.210
Great.

01:18:26.210 --> 01:18:28.730
So let's assume you
do that for GRPO.

01:18:28.730 --> 01:18:30.810
It was actually something
that the authors

01:18:30.810 --> 01:18:36.290
performed on math problems,
on reasoning problems.

01:18:36.290 --> 01:18:39.350
And what they saw was-- so
if you remember last week,

01:18:39.350 --> 01:18:44.090
we said that this was used
to incentivize the model

01:18:44.090 --> 01:18:48.430
to produce chains of reasoning
before producing an answer,

01:18:48.430 --> 01:18:51.450
and it allowed the
performance to increase.

01:18:51.450 --> 01:18:56.210
And one thing that is also
something that the authors saw

01:18:56.210 --> 01:19:00.290
was that the length
of the response

01:19:00.290 --> 01:19:05.380
tended to increase as you
were performing the training.

01:19:05.380 --> 01:19:08.340
And it even tended
to increase even

01:19:08.340 --> 01:19:12.660
when the performance of the
model was reaching a plateau.

01:19:12.660 --> 01:19:15.300
So on the left side, the
y-axis is the performance

01:19:15.300 --> 01:19:17.840
of the model for a math problem.

01:19:17.840 --> 01:19:25.060
And then on the right side, it
is the average response length.

01:19:25.060 --> 01:19:28.340
And so you may wonder,
well, why is that happening?

01:19:28.340 --> 01:19:33.260
Why would the model
incentivize the response

01:19:33.260 --> 01:19:37.060
from getting longer and longer
even when the performance is

01:19:37.060 --> 01:19:40.020
reaching a plateau?

01:19:40.020 --> 01:19:45.660
So this has to do with, again,
this big formula and how you are

01:19:45.660 --> 01:19:48.740
performing your normalization.

01:19:48.740 --> 01:19:55.300
So here I want you to focus on
just one piece of the slide.

01:19:55.300 --> 01:19:58.960
So do you see the 1 over
the response of the outputs?

01:19:58.960 --> 01:20:02.030
Do you see this term?

01:20:02.030 --> 01:20:09.870
So when responses are short,
this term, is it big or small?

01:20:09.870 --> 01:20:12.950
[INAUDIBLE]

01:20:12.950 --> 01:20:14.230
Big, yes.

01:20:14.230 --> 01:20:19.310
So when O is small, 1/O is big.

01:20:19.310 --> 01:20:22.590
How about when O
is big, is small.

01:20:22.590 --> 01:20:25.790
So this coefficient
is actually something

01:20:25.790 --> 01:20:30.230
that is multiplied with
the contribution of a token

01:20:30.230 --> 01:20:32.750
to the objective function.

01:20:32.750 --> 01:20:34.950
And here we're
saying that if you

01:20:34.950 --> 01:20:41.670
have a token that happens to
be in a short response, then

01:20:41.670 --> 01:20:45.810
the contribution will be bigger
than if it's in a long response.

01:20:48.910 --> 01:20:50.750
So far, nothing incredible.

01:20:50.750 --> 01:20:54.820
But now let's assume you
are thinking of it in terms

01:20:54.820 --> 01:20:57.070
of when the advantage is
positive versus the advantage

01:20:57.070 --> 01:20:59.400
is negative.

01:20:59.400 --> 01:21:04.200
So if you are in a
short output, then

01:21:04.200 --> 01:21:09.640
if your advantage
is positive, it

01:21:09.640 --> 01:21:13.160
will be a big
contribution upwards.

01:21:13.160 --> 01:21:17.340
If you are in a case
where it's negative,

01:21:17.340 --> 01:21:21.240
it will be a big
contribution downwards.

01:21:21.240 --> 01:21:24.860
And if you're in a long output,
if it's advantage positive,

01:21:24.860 --> 01:21:28.320
then it's a slight
upward increase.

01:21:28.320 --> 01:21:30.560
And if you're in a
negative advantage output,

01:21:30.560 --> 01:21:33.700
it's a slight
downward contribution.

01:21:33.700 --> 01:21:35.400
So what does that mean?

01:21:35.400 --> 01:21:40.440
So if you look at the
cases where you're actually

01:21:40.440 --> 01:21:46.360
outputting bad answers,
relatively speaking,

01:21:46.360 --> 01:21:49.720
the model will
prefer a bad answer

01:21:49.720 --> 01:21:56.160
that is longer compared to a
bad answer that is shorter.

01:21:56.160 --> 01:22:01.490
So if we go back here, what that
means is when the model comes

01:22:01.490 --> 01:22:04.890
to a situation where, OK, it
cannot do anything about the bad

01:22:04.890 --> 01:22:08.450
answers, it just
generates bad answers,

01:22:08.450 --> 01:22:11.830
it will then prefer
generating longer,

01:22:11.830 --> 01:22:16.570
bad answers compared
to shorter bad answers.

01:22:16.570 --> 01:22:20.310
And that is the reason
why in this graph,

01:22:20.310 --> 01:22:23.790
you are seeing a
plateau of performance,

01:22:23.790 --> 01:22:26.250
but the length that is
increasing because the model

01:22:26.250 --> 01:22:31.830
prefers longer bad responses
compared to shorter ones.

01:22:34.530 --> 01:22:37.610
Does everyone agree with this?

01:22:37.610 --> 01:22:39.110
And so why is this a problem?

01:22:39.110 --> 01:22:42.050
Well, it's a problem
because you don't want that.

01:22:42.050 --> 01:22:44.570
And that is one of
the motivations where

01:22:44.570 --> 01:22:46.610
there are some papers
that follow GRPO that

01:22:46.610 --> 01:22:49.330
try to tweak some little parts.

01:22:49.330 --> 01:22:51.770
This included the
normalization term.

01:22:51.770 --> 01:22:55.690
So here, in the case
of DAPO, it wanted

01:22:55.690 --> 01:22:58.160
to have the same
contribution for all tokens.

01:22:58.160 --> 01:23:00.420
So the normalization
is with respect

01:23:00.420 --> 01:23:02.460
to the sum of the outputs.

01:23:02.460 --> 01:23:05.853
And another one GRPO
done right, or Dr. GRPO.

01:23:05.853 --> 01:23:07.520
I don't know how you
would pronounce it,

01:23:07.520 --> 01:23:12.740
but they would just forego
of that normalization term.

01:23:12.740 --> 01:23:16.440
And I want to talk about
other problems that can occur.

01:23:16.440 --> 01:23:18.780
So the one other that
I want to mention

01:23:18.780 --> 01:23:22.500
is the way the
advantages are computed.

01:23:22.500 --> 01:23:26.280
They are divided by the standard
deviation of the rewards.

01:23:26.280 --> 01:23:29.700
You will have varying
standard deviations depending

01:23:29.700 --> 01:23:32.540
on how complex your input is.

01:23:32.540 --> 01:23:34.700
And that may introduce a bias.

01:23:34.700 --> 01:23:36.100
And that's not good.

01:23:36.100 --> 01:23:40.100
So you may want to remove
the standard deviation.

01:23:40.100 --> 01:23:42.300
And the second
one which I wanted

01:23:42.300 --> 01:23:46.020
to come back to the
point around epsilon

01:23:46.020 --> 01:23:51.100
is that if you
have an action that

01:23:51.100 --> 01:23:56.030
is of a very small
probability, and if you

01:23:56.030 --> 01:24:00.710
use the same epsilon
for both the lower

01:24:00.710 --> 01:24:03.790
bound and the upper bound,
you're actually not giving

01:24:03.790 --> 01:24:08.990
a chance for smaller
probability outputs

01:24:08.990 --> 01:24:11.250
to be generated even
significantly more.

01:24:11.250 --> 01:24:13.030
So let's assume your
probability is like,

01:24:13.030 --> 01:24:18.830
let's say, 0.001 and your
epsilon is, I don't know, 0.2,

01:24:18.830 --> 01:24:21.150
you only are giving a
small amount of margin

01:24:21.150 --> 01:24:24.470
for this to be actually
more discovered.

01:24:24.470 --> 01:24:26.850
And so it is not
encouraging diversity.

01:24:26.850 --> 01:24:29.830
So you may want
to choose epsilons

01:24:29.830 --> 01:24:35.190
that are different for the
lower bound and the upper bound.

01:24:35.190 --> 01:24:35.710
Cool.

01:24:35.710 --> 01:24:36.210
OK.

01:24:36.210 --> 01:24:37.710
I wanted to recap.

01:24:37.710 --> 01:24:43.430
GRPO, we saw that the gradients
are less noisy because they're

01:24:43.430 --> 01:24:47.830
computing advantages with
respect to the group average.

01:24:47.830 --> 01:24:51.150
And we saw that the
magnitude of policy updates

01:24:51.150 --> 01:24:54.960
are also handled by the
regular suspects when

01:24:54.960 --> 01:24:59.880
it comes to the methods,
so clipping and KL penalty.

01:24:59.880 --> 01:25:05.880
But we saw that we didn't have
explicit token-level credit

01:25:05.880 --> 01:25:09.320
assignments, and
that's not good.

01:25:09.320 --> 01:25:11.300
We don't have a value
function anymore,

01:25:11.300 --> 01:25:14.700
but we don't have token-level
credit assignments.

01:25:14.700 --> 01:25:16.440
And we want to solve that.

01:25:16.440 --> 01:25:20.760
And with that, I'll
give it to Shervin.

01:25:20.760 --> 01:25:22.480
Thank you, Afshin.

01:25:22.480 --> 01:25:25.040
So to illustrate
this point, we're

01:25:25.040 --> 01:25:27.120
going to go back to
our favorite example

01:25:27.120 --> 01:25:29.120
that we saw earlier
in the lecture

01:25:29.120 --> 01:25:33.640
regarding a Teddy bear that
moves within a sealed box.

01:25:33.640 --> 01:25:38.840
And we're going to recap what
GRPO and verifiable rewards

01:25:38.840 --> 01:25:40.120
incentivize.

01:25:40.120 --> 01:25:44.800
So here, regardless of the
reasoning steps, what matters

01:25:44.800 --> 01:25:47.840
was only the answer.

01:25:47.840 --> 01:25:49.140
So why is it a problem?

01:25:49.140 --> 01:25:51.890
As Afshin mentioned,
you are going

01:25:51.890 --> 01:25:55.350
to miss on the
intermediary steps.

01:25:55.350 --> 01:25:59.530
So here I showed what G
completions could give

01:25:59.530 --> 01:26:01.270
in terms of intermediary steps.

01:26:01.270 --> 01:26:03.770
So you could have a
combination of steps

01:26:03.770 --> 01:26:07.410
that go both well, steps that
go both wrong or just one

01:26:07.410 --> 01:26:09.850
of the two.

01:26:09.850 --> 01:26:15.890
And as you can see, every token
when you look at the formula

01:26:15.890 --> 01:26:20.690
is going to be associated
to the same end reward.

01:26:20.690 --> 01:26:23.390
So you're going to attribute
the same advantage as the end,

01:26:23.390 --> 01:26:28.050
even though the step is
wrong, which is one downside.

01:26:28.050 --> 01:26:31.950
And in the longer run,
so you might argue,

01:26:31.950 --> 01:26:36.570
OK, you might have completions
for which the intermediary steps

01:26:36.570 --> 01:26:40.250
don't make sense, but
on average they will.

01:26:40.250 --> 01:26:43.690
And on average, the
model will learn exactly

01:26:43.690 --> 01:26:46.050
which ones are good.

01:26:46.050 --> 01:26:50.170
But the thing is it's highly
inefficient because you see,

01:26:50.170 --> 01:26:54.230
you're not leveraging what
you can leverage at each step.

01:26:54.230 --> 01:26:58.010
So each of your terms
of your loss functions

01:26:58.010 --> 01:27:02.210
might not be correctly
rewarding the right things.

01:27:02.210 --> 01:27:04.130
So it's noisy locally.

01:27:04.130 --> 01:27:07.930
And then this phenomenon of
learning the correct behavior

01:27:07.930 --> 01:27:11.050
is one that is based
on the long-term.

01:27:11.050 --> 01:27:15.530
So this is not great.

01:27:15.530 --> 01:27:18.930
And Afshin just mentioned
a few minutes ago

01:27:18.930 --> 01:27:23.730
that in the GRPO formula you had
an advantage that was actually

01:27:23.730 --> 01:27:26.930
written out as a function
of T. So let's just

01:27:26.930 --> 01:27:30.930
suppose that we're not following
the common practice of being

01:27:30.930 --> 01:27:33.030
based on outcome supervision.

01:27:33.030 --> 01:27:36.290
Let's say you're on the process
supervision methodology,

01:27:36.290 --> 01:27:38.650
and you do follow that path.

01:27:38.650 --> 01:27:42.690
Then as we mentioned,
you need dedicated

01:27:42.690 --> 01:27:47.390
labeling for these token-level
advantage to make sense.

01:27:47.390 --> 01:27:51.500
And if you look at
the paper, they're

01:27:51.500 --> 01:27:53.500
slicing it at the
step level, which is

01:27:53.500 --> 01:27:54.880
here, what truly makes sense.

01:27:54.880 --> 01:27:59.660
So you need to label
each of them separately.

01:27:59.660 --> 01:28:05.060
And you might argue if
you go back to each step,

01:28:05.060 --> 01:28:09.300
that you could similarly have an
ambiguity of exactly what goes

01:28:09.300 --> 01:28:11.580
wrong in that step.

01:28:11.580 --> 01:28:16.040
So let's say you talk about
Teddy who works in the kitchen.

01:28:16.040 --> 01:28:21.260
Is the issue that Teddy works
or that he walks to the kitchen.

01:28:21.260 --> 01:28:27.080
So it could be a recursive
problem down to the token level.

01:28:27.080 --> 01:28:31.320
So you would be bound to
label token at the ends.

01:28:31.320 --> 01:28:36.900
And a reward on each token
might be a hard model to train.

01:28:36.900 --> 01:28:41.460
And as we mentioned here, one
of the reasons why we moved away

01:28:41.460 --> 01:28:43.900
from PPO in the first
place is to avoid

01:28:43.900 --> 01:28:47.350
maintaining the weights of
multiple models to train.

01:28:47.350 --> 01:28:51.150
So this is not
what we want to do.

01:28:51.150 --> 01:28:54.150
So if I had to recap
the benefits that we

01:28:54.150 --> 01:28:57.870
have in this reinforcement
learning stages

01:28:57.870 --> 01:29:02.310
that we want to keep, so we
want on-policy part to stay.

01:29:02.310 --> 01:29:08.230
So we like to train off of the
outputs of the model at stake.

01:29:08.230 --> 01:29:12.270
Then GRPO had these
downside of not

01:29:12.270 --> 01:29:15.870
crediting each token
individually, but rather

01:29:15.870 --> 01:29:18.830
the overall outcome and hoping
for the best on the things

01:29:18.830 --> 01:29:20.870
that we learn out of it.

01:29:20.870 --> 01:29:23.510
And we mentioned just
now that we do not

01:29:23.510 --> 01:29:27.110
want to train any additional
model to remedy that.

01:29:27.110 --> 01:29:33.110
So I wrote down a proposal of
what we could do to remedy it.

01:29:33.110 --> 01:29:38.310
So on-policy training is
typically us interested

01:29:38.310 --> 01:29:40.530
in the model's own generation.

01:29:40.530 --> 01:29:42.030
So that is set.

01:29:42.030 --> 01:29:47.000
One proposal is to train next
token prediction regarding

01:29:47.000 --> 01:29:48.700
the loss.

01:29:48.700 --> 01:29:54.320
And for the lack of additional
training as a constraint,

01:29:54.320 --> 01:29:58.960
maybe we could look around at
any strong models around there

01:29:58.960 --> 01:30:03.440
and then see what we
could learn from it.

01:30:03.440 --> 01:30:05.960
So I want to ask you a question.

01:30:05.960 --> 01:30:08.400
I talked about next
token prediction

01:30:08.400 --> 01:30:12.360
and a model that is stronger
than the one at stake.

01:30:12.360 --> 01:30:16.280
So is there a concept that
comes to mind as potentially

01:30:16.280 --> 01:30:17.220
attractive here?

01:30:21.940 --> 01:30:22.440
Yep?

01:30:22.440 --> 01:30:23.640
[INAUDIBLE]

01:30:23.640 --> 01:30:24.140
Yeah.

01:30:24.140 --> 01:30:25.820
So yeah the comment
is distillation.

01:30:25.820 --> 01:30:27.680
Yeah, exactly,
because distillation

01:30:27.680 --> 01:30:31.880
is about next token prediction,
transfer of information

01:30:31.880 --> 01:30:33.260
from one model to the other.

01:30:33.260 --> 01:30:34.840
Yes, spot on.

01:30:34.840 --> 01:30:36.520
But not the kind
that maybe you know.

01:30:36.520 --> 01:30:39.920
It's a new one.

01:30:39.920 --> 01:30:44.850
So before we do that, I'm going
to do a quick recap on the last

01:30:44.850 --> 01:30:47.610
that we use to
compare distributions

01:30:47.610 --> 01:30:49.890
of next token predictions,
which is the one that we're

01:30:49.890 --> 01:30:53.210
going to be interested
in, so this KL divergence.

01:30:53.210 --> 01:30:57.290
And I wanted to frame
it in very simple terms.

01:30:57.290 --> 01:31:02.450
So as you know, this loss
function is not symmetric.

01:31:02.450 --> 01:31:06.850
So KL divergence between
P and Q is not the same

01:31:06.850 --> 01:31:09.650
as the one between
Q and P. And if you

01:31:09.650 --> 01:31:16.250
want a way to know
instinctively what it means,

01:31:16.250 --> 01:31:18.730
so you look at the
sum, and you see

01:31:18.730 --> 01:31:22.890
that you sum based on weights
of the probability distribution

01:31:22.890 --> 01:31:24.850
of the first argument.

01:31:24.850 --> 01:31:27.370
So you're supposing
here that the world

01:31:27.370 --> 01:31:30.210
is governed by that
probability distribution.

01:31:30.210 --> 01:31:33.250
And then you have
the sum of logs

01:31:33.250 --> 01:31:39.650
of ratios that will
translate in penalization

01:31:39.650 --> 01:31:45.420
when the probability
weights of the two differ.

01:31:45.420 --> 01:31:51.020
So the way I propose we
interpret this, so KL of P and Q

01:31:51.020 --> 01:31:57.100
is in a world governed by P. How
bad is it to approximate with Q?

01:31:57.100 --> 01:31:59.780
So typically when
you do distillation,

01:31:59.780 --> 01:32:05.000
you would govern the world
with the teacher model.

01:32:05.000 --> 01:32:06.460
So T would be the
first argument,

01:32:06.460 --> 01:32:09.220
and the student would
be the second argument.

01:32:09.220 --> 01:32:13.260
But here I said we want to
do it on policy with respect

01:32:13.260 --> 01:32:15.340
to the students.

01:32:15.340 --> 01:32:18.260
So one natural way
to formulate this

01:32:18.260 --> 01:32:24.400
is not to do KL divergence of T
and S, but rather the opposite.

01:32:24.400 --> 01:32:29.140
And this is called the
reverse KL divergence.

01:32:29.140 --> 01:32:34.340
And it has some interesting
properties attached to it.

01:32:34.340 --> 01:32:40.700
So when you look
at the sum and you

01:32:40.700 --> 01:32:44.630
look at the distribution of
the student and the teacher,

01:32:44.630 --> 01:32:52.790
you see that when T is not equal
to 0 and if S is close to 0,

01:32:52.790 --> 01:32:56.830
then the contributions
of these terms to the sum

01:32:56.830 --> 01:32:58.550
are not that big.

01:32:58.550 --> 01:33:02.950
So we say they are forgiving
to these mismatches

01:33:02.950 --> 01:33:06.390
between these two distributions.

01:33:06.390 --> 01:33:13.790
But on the other hand, if ti
for a given term is small,

01:33:13.790 --> 01:33:17.030
but SI is nonzero,
then you're going

01:33:17.030 --> 01:33:21.710
to have this ratio that
is going to be very big.

01:33:21.710 --> 01:33:24.810
And that is associated to
a weight that is nonzero.

01:33:24.810 --> 01:33:29.070
So you're going to
get a huge term.

01:33:29.070 --> 01:33:36.110
And these latter points combined
with a typical student model's

01:33:36.110 --> 01:33:39.430
architecture to not have
as many degrees of freedom

01:33:39.430 --> 01:33:43.200
as the teacher one to
make it be mode seeking,

01:33:43.200 --> 01:33:45.880
meaning that the peaks in the
distribution of the teacher

01:33:45.880 --> 01:33:51.600
will be those that will be
learned by the students.

01:33:51.600 --> 01:33:56.480
So these did this
make sense here,

01:33:56.480 --> 01:34:01.320
the fact that we use
reverse KL divergence?

01:34:01.320 --> 01:34:03.520
So I'm asking the question
because even though it's

01:34:03.520 --> 01:34:06.560
what people use today,
the original paper

01:34:06.560 --> 01:34:07.940
did a mix of both.

01:34:07.940 --> 01:34:12.240
So I think it's not obvious that
this is obviously the better

01:34:12.240 --> 01:34:15.320
one, but--

01:34:15.320 --> 01:34:17.760
So now let's go back
to our favorite example

01:34:17.760 --> 01:34:21.880
and apply this way of
measuring the loss based

01:34:21.880 --> 01:34:24.520
on the distributions
of student and teacher.

01:34:24.520 --> 01:34:27.920
So here what we want to do
in on-policy distillation

01:34:27.920 --> 01:34:33.840
is that we want the student
to generate a completion based

01:34:33.840 --> 01:34:34.940
on a prompt.

01:34:34.940 --> 01:34:39.170
And then we ask the teacher
based on the standard context,

01:34:39.170 --> 01:34:42.210
how much does the token
generated by the students

01:34:42.210 --> 01:34:43.810
make sense to you?

01:34:43.810 --> 01:34:48.090
And we ask this question at
each new generated token.

01:34:48.090 --> 01:34:51.530
So let's say you have this
prompt with the problem

01:34:51.530 --> 01:34:54.450
statement of Teddy
in a sealed box.

01:34:54.450 --> 01:34:56.730
And you ask, where is Teddy?

01:34:56.730 --> 01:34:59.510
So let's say the sentence
starts with Teddy.

01:34:59.510 --> 01:35:01.550
So Teddy makes sense
to the teacher.

01:35:01.550 --> 01:35:03.170
I would generate also Teddy.

01:35:03.170 --> 01:35:06.570
But then the student
says, Teddy climbs.

01:35:06.570 --> 01:35:09.050
And the teacher
would say, OK, wait.

01:35:09.050 --> 01:35:15.050
Climbs is not a high-probability
token in my distribution.

01:35:15.050 --> 01:35:18.970
So this is what the
teacher would penalize here

01:35:18.970 --> 01:35:23.450
and what the reverse KL
divergence would penalize and so

01:35:23.450 --> 01:35:24.290
on.

01:35:24.290 --> 01:35:28.850
Do you see anything that is
not being penalized here.

01:35:28.850 --> 01:35:31.070
And that is a surprise
to you in this example?

01:35:31.070 --> 01:35:33.690
So the things that are
penalized in this toy example

01:35:33.690 --> 01:35:36.030
are things in red.

01:35:36.030 --> 01:35:36.530
Yeah?

01:35:36.530 --> 01:35:37.030
[INAUDIBLE]

01:35:37.030 --> 01:35:38.440
So the suggestion is work.

01:35:38.440 --> 01:35:43.120
So in O2 why is works
not penalized either?

01:35:43.120 --> 01:35:45.060
So do you have an
explanation for it?

01:35:45.060 --> 01:35:48.700
Or does it make sense
that it's not penalized?

01:35:48.700 --> 01:35:49.600
Yeah?

01:35:49.600 --> 01:35:51.900
[INAUDIBLE]

01:35:51.900 --> 01:35:54.320
So yeah, the intuition is great.

01:35:54.320 --> 01:35:56.580
But the answer was
maybe the teacher

01:35:56.580 --> 01:35:59.520
interprets the Teddy
bear as being a human.

01:35:59.520 --> 01:36:04.320
So as we said, once you
generate the next token,

01:36:04.320 --> 01:36:07.720
all the tokens generated
before come into the context.

01:36:07.720 --> 01:36:10.180
So let's say you
are a teacher model.

01:36:10.180 --> 01:36:15.140
Now in your context that Teddy
can do things like climbing.

01:36:15.140 --> 01:36:18.360
Then walking might be in
the span of possibilities.

01:36:18.360 --> 01:36:22.540
So it's not something
that is super surprising.

01:36:22.540 --> 01:36:26.900
So that is one property
that is interesting.

01:36:26.900 --> 01:36:31.100
And yeah, that's all
I had to say here.

01:36:31.100 --> 01:36:36.190
So when we look at
on-policy distillation,

01:36:36.190 --> 01:36:41.110
we see that we have this
supervision signal that is dense

01:36:41.110 --> 01:36:44.710
because it comes at the token
level instead of the outcome

01:36:44.710 --> 01:36:45.990
level.

01:36:45.990 --> 01:36:52.010
And contrary to SFT, as the
name suggests, on-policy,

01:36:52.010 --> 01:36:54.430
meaning that you're
training the model

01:36:54.430 --> 01:37:00.310
to be better at with respect
to its own completions.

01:37:00.310 --> 01:37:03.750
And you see in practice
great results coming from it.

01:37:03.750 --> 01:37:05.850
And when you go even
further than that,

01:37:05.850 --> 01:37:08.270
with the same amount
of compute, you

01:37:08.270 --> 01:37:13.630
see that you get more bang out
of your buck from this method.

01:37:13.630 --> 01:37:20.950
And also it is more friendly to
parameter constraint situations.

01:37:20.950 --> 01:37:24.590
So there is this great blog
post by Thinking Machine

01:37:24.590 --> 01:37:28.270
that did some toy
ablation studies.

01:37:28.270 --> 01:37:32.270
And for example, if you play
with the rank of the weights

01:37:32.270 --> 01:37:35.560
that you want to
tune and you do SFT

01:37:35.560 --> 01:37:38.280
first and on-policy
distillation next,

01:37:38.280 --> 01:37:42.880
you see that the delta between
the performance that you

01:37:42.880 --> 01:37:47.200
get after SFT is
shortened greatly

01:37:47.200 --> 01:37:50.600
after you apply on-policy
distillation, which

01:37:50.600 --> 01:37:54.520
shows that the method
is great at operating

01:37:54.520 --> 01:37:55.900
under compute constraints.

01:37:59.920 --> 01:38:01.840
OK, great.

01:38:01.840 --> 01:38:04.580
And also, since there
is no reward model,

01:38:04.580 --> 01:38:06.082
there is nothing to hack.

01:38:06.082 --> 01:38:07.540
And if there was
something to hack,

01:38:07.540 --> 01:38:08.860
it would be more than happy to.

01:38:08.860 --> 01:38:13.440
Because if on-policy decision
wants to bring the loss down

01:38:13.440 --> 01:38:16.300
to 0, then you would align
with the teacher distribution,

01:38:16.300 --> 01:38:19.520
which is a good edge case.

01:38:19.520 --> 01:38:21.480
That is great.

01:38:21.480 --> 01:38:26.080
But then when I mentioned
we need a teacher model,

01:38:26.080 --> 01:38:28.760
then you need to have
that in the first place.

01:38:28.760 --> 01:38:33.315
Let's say you're
training a model,

01:38:33.315 --> 01:38:34.440
then you need to have that.

01:38:34.440 --> 01:38:35.982
And you're training
a frontier model.

01:38:35.982 --> 01:38:40.840
Maybe you're the best, so there
is no one that could teach you.

01:38:40.840 --> 01:38:46.260
And then the second thing
is that contrary to GRPO

01:38:46.260 --> 01:38:50.000
where the signal that you're
basing your updates on

01:38:50.000 --> 01:38:54.760
is true labels, now
you're comparing yourself

01:38:54.760 --> 01:38:58.700
to a teacher models
output distribution.

01:38:58.700 --> 01:39:01.560
And it might not be
perfect, nor might it

01:39:01.560 --> 01:39:05.040
be complete in terms of
coverage on the domains

01:39:05.040 --> 01:39:06.720
that you're interested in.

01:39:06.720 --> 01:39:11.440
Which is why it might
also come as a limitation.

01:39:11.440 --> 01:39:12.500
Does that make sense?

01:39:15.400 --> 01:39:16.540
OK, great.

01:39:16.540 --> 01:39:19.120
So now I'm going to see
each of these limitations

01:39:19.120 --> 01:39:20.240
more in detail.

01:39:20.240 --> 01:39:22.960
So do you see a
way where we could

01:39:22.960 --> 01:39:27.360
avoid the need for a teacher?

01:39:27.360 --> 01:39:30.120
OK, I know it's not obvious,
but once you're going to see it,

01:39:30.120 --> 01:39:31.590
it sounds magical.

01:39:31.590 --> 01:39:35.530
So you just need to
recall that everything

01:39:35.530 --> 01:39:40.450
that you're inputting for you to
obtain the output distributions

01:39:40.450 --> 01:39:42.850
is done by teacher forcing way.

01:39:42.850 --> 01:39:47.810
So the figure that
Afshin had, I think here

01:39:47.810 --> 01:39:52.010
between generation and training,
you would be on the right side,

01:39:52.010 --> 01:39:56.170
where once you generate the
tokens from your student model,

01:39:56.170 --> 01:39:59.270
you put all of
them, the prompts,

01:39:59.270 --> 01:40:03.010
plus the tokens from the student
model as input of your teacher.

01:40:03.010 --> 01:40:06.850
And you look at the output
next token prediction

01:40:06.850 --> 01:40:08.890
of your teacher model.

01:40:08.890 --> 01:40:12.370
So nothing prevents you
from injecting things

01:40:12.370 --> 01:40:17.130
that could help a potential
teacher as part of the prompt,

01:40:17.130 --> 01:40:20.050
including the answer
and everything that

01:40:20.050 --> 01:40:21.690
leads up to the answer.

01:40:21.690 --> 01:40:25.170
So this is what the
self-distillation paper does.

01:40:25.170 --> 01:40:27.330
So you have a prompt.

01:40:27.330 --> 01:40:30.380
Then you have the
complete answer.

01:40:30.380 --> 01:40:35.740
And you ask the model to come
back with an answer once again.

01:40:35.740 --> 01:40:39.500
And this is just a trick for
you to get this nice probability

01:40:39.500 --> 01:40:42.940
distributions ready to
compare with a student.

01:40:42.940 --> 01:40:44.660
And the nice thing
here is that you can

01:40:44.660 --> 01:40:46.400
do that with the same model.

01:40:49.300 --> 01:40:51.700
Does that look magical?

01:40:51.700 --> 01:40:52.600
Yeah?

01:40:52.600 --> 01:40:55.380
Yeah, I know, it looks great.

01:40:55.380 --> 01:40:55.880
OK.

01:40:55.880 --> 01:40:57.660
So the second thing
that we mentioned

01:40:57.660 --> 01:41:00.160
is the-- oh, yeah,
there was a question?

01:41:00.160 --> 01:41:01.860
[INAUDIBLE]

01:41:01.860 --> 01:41:04.740
So the question is,
if I had to summarize

01:41:04.740 --> 01:41:08.440
is, when would you benefit most
from on-policy distillation?

01:41:08.440 --> 01:41:10.900
So yeah, you're
right that let's say

01:41:10.900 --> 01:41:13.260
you're the best model
out there, maybe

01:41:13.260 --> 01:41:17.920
you will have a lesser
benefit from this method.

01:41:17.920 --> 01:41:20.020
And maybe you would
use on-policy decision

01:41:20.020 --> 01:41:23.100
for other needs.

01:41:23.100 --> 01:41:27.580
The GLM 5 paper that we saw
last week is one example where

01:41:27.580 --> 01:41:31.430
you would use it to calibrate
the fact that you do not

01:41:31.430 --> 01:41:34.170
lose knowledge that you
have learned in the past,

01:41:34.170 --> 01:41:37.550
so you would distill yourself
on previously gained knowledge.

01:41:37.550 --> 01:41:38.610
But you're right.

01:41:38.610 --> 01:41:46.490
You would typically use it on
faster models of your series.

01:41:46.490 --> 01:41:49.050
So let's say you have
your frontier lab.

01:41:49.050 --> 01:41:50.840
You have a pro
and a flash model.

01:41:50.840 --> 01:41:52.590
Maybe on-policy
distillation would be what

01:41:52.590 --> 01:41:54.050
you use on your flash series.

01:41:54.050 --> 01:41:59.250
And then your pro series
might be more foundational

01:41:59.250 --> 01:42:02.550
GRPO and then these
core techniques

01:42:02.550 --> 01:42:06.430
that might need more compute
but that are based off

01:42:06.430 --> 01:42:09.070
hardcore labels.

01:42:09.070 --> 01:42:09.670
Thank you.

01:42:09.670 --> 01:42:10.990
Yep.

01:42:10.990 --> 01:42:12.110
Awesome.

01:42:12.110 --> 01:42:15.630
So the last thing that I wanted
to mention is on the fact

01:42:15.630 --> 01:42:19.030
that you might be bounded
by the teacher's abilities.

01:42:19.030 --> 01:42:25.030
So one approach
is to take a model

01:42:25.030 --> 01:42:30.080
and then use these verifiable
rewards across domains.

01:42:30.080 --> 01:42:33.080
And then have experts
for each domain.

01:42:33.080 --> 01:42:36.360
And then when you want to do
these on-policy distillation,

01:42:36.360 --> 01:42:41.680
do it against the
expert of that domain.

01:42:41.680 --> 01:42:46.057
So this is why there is a multi
teacher on policy distillation

01:42:46.057 --> 01:42:47.140
that comes in the picture.

01:42:47.140 --> 01:42:49.200
So now is it a
surprise to anyone

01:42:49.200 --> 01:42:53.760
that we need multiple teachers
here and not just one?

01:42:53.760 --> 01:42:58.640
Well, empirically if you do
these post-training RL on just

01:42:58.640 --> 01:43:00.760
a single one, you
might have things

01:43:00.760 --> 01:43:03.360
like forgetting
that happens and you

01:43:03.360 --> 01:43:06.080
might use a given
set of parameters

01:43:06.080 --> 01:43:08.080
towards multiple objectives.

01:43:08.080 --> 01:43:15.940
So this is a method that has
shown to be more efficient.

01:43:15.940 --> 01:43:16.440
OK.

01:43:16.440 --> 01:43:16.942
Awesome.

01:43:16.942 --> 01:43:18.900
And with that, I hope
you have a great weekend.

01:43:18.900 --> 01:43:22.250
[APPLAUSE]

